window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["jenkins"]=[{"id":"jenkins-f0001","card":"q-jenkins-001-what-jenkins-is-and-the-devops-lifecycle","sec":"jenkins-s002","q":"What is Jenkins, and what role does it play in a CI/CD pipeline?","a":"<p>Jenkins is an open-source automation server written in Java. In CI/CD it is the orchestrator: it watches source control, builds and tests code on every change (CI), and automates packaging, artifact publishing and deployment through stages to various environments (CD). It ties together compilers, test runners, scanners and deploy tools via plugins and pipelines.</p>\n<p>The DevOps lifecycle is drawn as an infinity symbol: a continuous loop until the system is decommissioned. It has two groups of stages:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Development: Plan, Code, Build, Test.</li>\n  <li>Operational: Release, Deploy, Operate, Monitor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Jenkins automates the Build, Test, Release and Deploy stages.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Integration</strong> ties to Build and Test. The goal is to find and fix issues early. Jenkins builds the code into an artifact (container image, Java archive, Windows executable), runs automated tests, and lets the artifact move on only when the tests pass.</li>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Delivery/Deployment</strong> ties to Release and Deploy. Release means uploading artifacts (push a container image to a repository, publish a JAR). Deploy can be manual or fully automated (Continuous Deployment), where Jenkins executes the deployment instructions with little or no human interaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Plan&#x27;) {\n            steps { echo &#x27;Planning the application...&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Code&#x27;) {\n            steps { git &#x27;https://github.com/example/repo.git&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;mvn clean package&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Test&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;mvn test&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Release&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;docker push my-app:latest&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml&#x27; }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0002","card":"q-jenkins-001-what-jenkins-is-and-the-devops-lifecycle","sec":"jenkins-s002","q":"Which DevOps lifecycle stages does Jenkins automate, and which stages stay outside its scope?","a":"<p>Jenkins automates Build, Test, Release and Deploy. Plan and Code happen in issue trackers and source control. Operate and Monitor are handled by runtime platforms and tools such as Prometheus or CloudWatch. Jenkins can still trigger or call these tools, but it does not replace them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0003","card":"q-jenkins-001-what-jenkins-is-and-the-devops-lifecycle","sec":"jenkins-s002","q":"In a Jenkins pipeline, why is the artifact built once in the Build stage and then promoted, instead of being rebuilt for each environment?","a":"<p>Building once guarantees that the binary tested in staging is the same binary that reaches production. Rebuilding per environment can produce different outputs because of changed dependencies or tool versions, so test results would no longer prove anything about what was deployed. Environment differences belong in configuration, not in the build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0004","card":"q-jenkins-001-what-jenkins-is-and-the-devops-lifecycle","sec":"jenkins-s002","q":"In a Jenkins pipeline, what is the difference between the Release stage and the Deploy stage?","a":"<p>Release publishes the versioned artifact to a repository (here a container registry). Deploy makes a running environment use it (here <code>kubectl apply</code> updates the cluster). Separating them lets you keep a stored, immutable artifact that you can deploy, roll back or redeploy later without rebuilding. Tagging with <code>latest</code> is a weak practice, because it is mutable; use a version or commit SHA.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Release&#x27;) {\n    steps { sh &#x27;docker push my-app:latest&#x27; }\n}\nstage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;) {\n    steps { sh &#x27;kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml&#x27; }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0005","card":"q-jenkins-001-what-jenkins-is-and-the-devops-lifecycle","sec":"jenkins-s002","q":"Why is a <code>mvn clean package</code> style build command run in a CI server instead of only on developer laptops?","a":"<p>A CI server builds from a clean checkout in a controlled environment, so results do not depend on a developer's local tools, cached files or uncommitted changes. It also gives every change the same checks and a shared, visible result, which catches integration problems early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0006","card":"q-jenkins-002-ci-vs-continuous-delivery-vs-continuous","sec":"jenkins-s003","q":"What is the difference between Continuous Integration, Continuous Delivery, and Continuous Deployment?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>CI</strong>: developers merge frequently; each change is automatically built and tested.</li>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Delivery</strong>: every passing build is automatically prepared and deployable, but a human approves the release to production.</li>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Deployment</strong>: every passing change is released to production automatically, with no manual gate.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0007","card":"q-jenkins-002-ci-vs-continuous-delivery-vs-continuous","sec":"jenkins-s003","q":"Your company must have a human sign-off before every production release, but wants production-ready builds after every merge. Which practice fits, and how do you model it in Jenkins?","a":"<p>Continuous Delivery. Every passing change is built, tested and made deployable automatically, and a person approves the final production step. In Jenkins you model the approval with an <code>input</code> step before the production stage, usually wrapped in a <code>timeout</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0008","card":"q-jenkins-002-ci-vs-continuous-delivery-vs-continuous","sec":"jenkins-s003","q":"What practices must be in place before a team can safely move from Continuous Delivery to Continuous Deployment?","a":"<p>Fast and reliable automated tests with good coverage, automated rollback or safe release methods (feature flags, canary or blue/green), monitoring with alerting that can stop a bad release, small and frequent changes, and database or config migrations that are backward compatible. Without these, removing the manual gate only moves failures to production faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0009","card":"q-jenkins-002-ci-vs-continuous-delivery-vs-continuous","sec":"jenkins-s003","q":"A team says it does CI, but developers merge to the main branch once a month. What is wrong?","a":"<p>Continuous Integration is defined by frequent integration (at least daily), not by having a build server. Monthly merges mean long-lived branches, large merge conflicts and late discovery of integration bugs. A CI server alone does not give CI benefits if changes are not integrated often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0010","card":"q-jenkins-003-jenkins-vs-github-actions","sec":"jenkins-s004","q":"What are the main advantages of GitHub Actions over tools like Jenkins or CircleCI?","a":"<p>GitHub integration, easy YAML setup, the action marketplace, a free tier and scalable runners.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0011","card":"q-jenkins-003-jenkins-vs-github-actions","sec":"jenkins-s004","q":"When would you pick Jenkins over GitHub Actions for a CI/CD system?","a":"<p>Pick Jenkins when you need full control and self-hosting (air-gapped or regulated environments), a very large plugin ecosystem, existing investment in Jenkinsfiles and shared libraries, SCM-agnostic pipelines across several systems, or custom agents on special hardware. Pick GitHub Actions when code already lives in GitHub and you want low maintenance and quick YAML setup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0012","card":"q-jenkins-003-jenkins-vs-github-actions","sec":"jenkins-s004","q":"What operational costs come with Jenkins that a hosted CI service such as GitHub Actions removes?","a":"<p>With Jenkins you run and patch the controller, upgrade the JVM and plugins, handle plugin compatibility, back up <code>JENKINS_HOME</code>, scale agents and secure the system. A hosted service takes over most of this, in exchange for less control and per-minute pricing for private repositories or larger runners.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0013","card":"q-jenkins-004-controller-and-agent-architecture","sec":"jenkins-s005","q":"Explain the controller-agent architecture in Jenkins.","a":"<p>The <strong>controller</strong> hosts the UI, schedules builds, stores config and job history, and manages plugins. <strong>Agents</strong> (nodes) are separate machines or containers that run the build workloads. The controller dispatches jobs to agents based on labels and available executors. Best practice: run no builds on the controller; keep it for orchestration only.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0014","card":"q-jenkins-004-controller-and-agent-architecture","sec":"jenkins-s005","q":"Why should you avoid running build workloads on the controller?","a":"<p>The controller holds all state, credentials and orchestration. Running builds there risks resource exhaustion (OOM/CPU), lets build scripts read sensitive controller files and secrets, and can crash the whole instance. Set controller executors to 0 and run all builds on agents.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0015","card":"q-jenkins-004-controller-and-agent-architecture","sec":"jenkins-s005","q":"What happens to a queued build when no agent with the requested label is online, and how do you diagnose it?","a":"<p>The build stays in the queue and Jenkins shows a message like \"Waiting for next available executor\" or \"There are no nodes with the label\". Check that the agent is online, that the label spelling in <code>agent { label '...' }</code> matches, that the agent has a free executor, and that the node is not restricted to specific jobs only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0016","card":"q-jenkins-004-controller-and-agent-architecture","sec":"jenkins-s005","q":"What are the benefits and the single point of failure in a Jenkins controller and agent design, and how do you reduce the risk?","a":"<p>Benefits: builds scale horizontally, agents can have different OSes and tools, and the controller stays stable. The controller is still a single point of failure, because it holds scheduling, configuration and history. Reduce the risk with regular <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> backups, configuration as code, running the controller on a durable volume with fast restore, and ephemeral agents that are easy to recreate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0017","card":"q-jenkins-004-controller-and-agent-architecture","sec":"jenkins-s005","q":"How would you scale a Jenkins setup that has long build queues at peak hours?","a":"<p>Add agents, ideally dynamic ones that start on demand (Kubernetes pods or EC2 instances) and are removed when idle. Use labels so jobs land on suitable agents, run independent stages in <code>parallel</code>, and keep the controller at zero executors. Check whether the queue is caused by too few executors, a shared resource lock, or slow builds that need caching.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0018","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"What is a Jenkins executor, and how many per agent?","a":"<p>An executor is a slot on a node that runs one build at a time; a node with N executors runs N concurrent builds. Rule of thumb: about one executor per CPU core, fewer for memory-heavy or I/O-heavy builds. Too many causes contention or OOM; too few wastes capacity. Keep the controller's executors at 0.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0019","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"What are Jenkins labels?","a":"<p>Tags assigned to agents (for example <code>linux</code>, <code>docker</code>, <code>maven</code>). A pipeline requests a label so the build runs only on matching agents. Labels support expressions such as <code>linux &amp;&amp; docker</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n  agent { label &#x27;linux &amp;&amp; docker&#x27; }   // run only on agents with both labels\n  stages { stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;make&#x27; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0020","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"How does Jenkins authenticate agents, and which protocols are used?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>SSH</strong>: the controller connects out to the agent using SSH credentials (controller-initiated).</li>\n  <li><strong>Inbound (JNLP)</strong>: the agent connects in to the controller using a secret or agent token (agent-initiated; good behind NAT or firewalls).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Agents are validated through these secrets and keys, and you can also lock down which agent commands the controller trusts.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0021","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"What are the default ports?","a":"<p>Web UI: 8080 (HTTP). The inbound (JNLP) agent port is configured separately (often 50000) under Manage Jenkins, Security; it can be fixed or random.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0022","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"In Jenkins, why is the inbound agent connection often the better choice when agents sit behind NAT or a firewall?","a":"<p>With an inbound agent the agent opens the connection to the controller, so you only need outbound access from the agent. With SSH launch the controller must reach the agent, which needs inbound firewall rules or a public address. Since Jenkins 2.217 inbound agents can also use WebSocket, which avoids opening a separate TCP port on the controller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0023","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"In Jenkins, what is the practical trade-off between SSH launch and inbound agents for security and management?","a":"<p>SSH launch is controller-initiated: central credentials, no secret on the agent, and the controller decides when to connect. Inbound agents keep a secret on the agent machine and need a way to start the agent process, but they work across network boundaries. Pick SSH when the controller can reach the machines, inbound otherwise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0024","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"In a Jenkins pipeline, what does this <code>agent</code> line do when no agent matches both labels, and how is it different from <code>agent any</code>?","a":"<p>Jenkins waits for an agent that has both the <code>linux</code> and <code>docker</code> labels. The build does not fall back to another agent. <code>agent any</code> accepts any available executor, which is easy but gives no guarantee about tools or OS. Labels make the requirement explicit.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent { label &#x27;linux &amp;&amp; docker&#x27; }   // run only on agents with both labels</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0025","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"How many executors should a Jenkins agent have, and what symptoms show that the number is wrong?","a":"<p>Start around one executor per CPU core and lower it for memory-heavy or I/O-heavy builds. Too many executors cause slow builds, timeouts and out-of-memory kills. Too few leave CPU idle while the queue grows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0026","card":"q-jenkins-006-freestyle-vs-pipeline-jobs-and-plugins","sec":"jenkins-s007","q":"What is the difference between a Freestyle job and a Pipeline job?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Freestyle</strong>: configured in the GUI, roughly single-stage; simple but limited, and the config lives inside Jenkins (harder to version and review).</li>\n  <li><strong>Pipeline</strong>: defined as code in a <code>Jenkinsfile</code> (Groovy); supports multi-stage flows, parallelism, <code>when</code> and <code>input</code>; lives in source control (auditable, reusable, portable).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0027","card":"q-jenkins-006-freestyle-vs-pipeline-jobs-and-plugins","sec":"jenkins-s007","q":"What are Jenkins plugins, and how do they extend core functionality?","a":"<p>Plugins are add-ons for SCM integrations (Git), build tools, pipeline steps, credential providers, cloud agents (Kubernetes, EC2), notifications and more. Jenkins core is intentionally minimal; almost all real functionality comes from plugins, managed under Manage Jenkins, Plugins.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0028","card":"q-jenkins-006-freestyle-vs-pipeline-jobs-and-plugins","sec":"jenkins-s007","q":"Why can a Pipeline job survive a Jenkins controller restart while a long Freestyle build cannot continue?","a":"<p>Pipeline runs persist their execution state to disk, so after a restart Jenkins can resume the build (shell steps running on agents may keep running). A Freestyle build runs as a single in-memory process, so a restart aborts it. Durability settings of Pipeline trade performance for how much state is written.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0029","card":"q-jenkins-006-freestyle-vs-pipeline-jobs-and-plugins","sec":"jenkins-s007","q":"What risks come from installing many Jenkins plugins, and how do you manage them?","a":"<p>Plugins can have security vulnerabilities, conflicting dependencies, abandoned maintainers and slow startup. Manage them by installing only what you need, pinning versions in a plugin list or JCasC and image build, testing upgrades on a staging controller, reading the security advisories, and removing unused plugins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0030","card":"q-jenkins-006-freestyle-vs-pipeline-jobs-and-plugins","sec":"jenkins-s007","q":"Your team has 200 Freestyle jobs configured in the GUI. Why is that a maintenance problem?","a":"<p>The configuration lives only in <code>JENKINS_HOME</code>, so there is no code review, no history of who changed what, no easy rollback, and copy-paste drift between jobs. Pipeline as code with shared libraries makes the same logic versioned and reusable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0031","card":"q-jenkins-007-jenkins-home-configuration-and-backup","sec":"jenkins-s008","q":"What is <code>JENKINS_HOME</code>, and what lives in it?","a":"<p>The root data directory holding all Jenkins state:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>config.xml</code>: global config</li>\n  <li><code>jobs/</code>: per-job config and build history</li>\n  <li><code>plugins/</code>: installed plugins</li>\n  <li><code>secrets/</code>: encryption keys and credential material</li>\n  <li><code>users/</code>, <code>nodes/</code>, <code>workspace/</code>, <code>fingerprints/</code>, <code>updates/</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Backing it up backs up the whole instance.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0032","card":"q-jenkins-007-jenkins-home-configuration-and-backup","sec":"jenkins-s008","q":"How does Jenkins store configuration, and why XML?","a":"<p>Config is persisted as XML files on disk (<code>config.xml</code>, per-job <code>config.xml</code>, node and user configs). Jenkins serializes its Java object model with XStream, so the files are human-readable, diffable and directly editable. That is also why config can be version-controlled and reloaded.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0033","card":"q-jenkins-007-jenkins-home-configuration-and-backup","sec":"jenkins-s008","q":"Why should <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> live on a separate, persistent volume, and what would you consider excluding from its backup?","a":"<p>A separate volume lets you replace or rebuild the controller machine without losing jobs, history and credentials, and lets you snapshot it. Backups usually exclude <code>workspace/</code>, caches and large build logs or artifacts, because they are re-creatable or stored elsewhere and make backups huge.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0034","card":"q-jenkins-007-jenkins-home-configuration-and-backup","sec":"jenkins-s008","q":"Why is the <code>secrets/</code> directory of Jenkins sensitive, and what goes wrong if you restore <code>credentials.xml</code> without it?","a":"<p><code>secrets/</code> holds the keys (for example <code>master.key</code> and <code>hudson.util.Secret</code>) that encrypt stored credentials. <code>credentials.xml</code> contains values encrypted with those keys, so a restore without the matching keys leaves unreadable credentials. Anyone who gets both the file and the keys can decrypt the secrets, so protect backups accordingly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0035","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"How do you back up and restore a Jenkins instance?","a":"<p>Manual: stop or quiesce Jenkins and archive <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> (at minimum <code>config.xml</code>, <code>jobs/</code>, <code>users/</code>, <code>secrets/</code>, <code>credentials.xml</code>, <code>plugins/</code>). To restore, put it back and restart. With plugins: <strong>ThinBackup</strong> (scheduled full or incremental), or keep config in JCasC plus jobs as code so the instance is reproducible.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0036","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"How do you move jobs from one Jenkins instance to another?","a":"<p>Copy the job folder under <code>jobs/&lt;name&gt;/</code> (with <code>config.xml</code>) to the new controller and reload; use the Jenkins CLI (<code>get-job</code> / <code>create-job</code>); use the Job Import plugin; or, best, keep jobs as <code>Jenkinsfile</code> and JCasC so migration is just pointing the new controller at the repos.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0037","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"What is the difference between soft reload (<code>/reload</code>) and safe restart (<code>/safeRestart</code>)?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>/reload</code> re-reads config from disk into memory without restarting the JVM (fast; use after editing config on disk).</li>\n  <li><code>/safeRestart</code> waits for running builds to finish, then restarts the JVM, so no builds are killed.</li>\n  <li><code>/restart</code> restarts immediately.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0038","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"What is the System Log for, and where do you configure custom log recorders?","a":"<p>The System Log surfaces controller and plugin logging for diagnostics. Under Manage Jenkins, System Log you can add custom Log Recorders that capture specific loggers (by package or class) at chosen levels, which helps debug one plugin without drowning in global logs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0039","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"When would you use <code>/safeRestart</code> instead of <code>/restart</code>, and what is the downside of <code>/safeRestart</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>/safeRestart</code> after plugin installs or updates in a busy system, because it lets running builds finish and queues new ones. The downside is waiting: a long-running or stuck build can delay the restart indefinitely. <code>/restart</code> is immediate and kills running builds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0040","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"You moved a Jenkins instance by copying only the <code>jobs/</code> directory to a new controller. What will be missing or broken?","a":"<p>Credentials, users, global configuration, node definitions and plugins are not there. Job configs may reference plugins that are not installed and credential IDs that do not exist. Migrate <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> as a whole (including <code>secrets/</code>) or rebuild with JCasC, plugin lists and jobs as code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0041","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"How do you make Jenkins backups reliable and test them?","a":"<p>Take scheduled backups of <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> or volume snapshots, store them off the machine, protect the <code>secrets/</code> content, and keep configuration in code. Test by restoring into a separate instance and checking that jobs, credentials and plugins work. A backup that has never been restored is not proven.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0042","card":"q-jenkins-009-requirements-and-where-to-host","sec":"jenkins-s010","q":"Why can a Jenkins server on a laptop not receive GitHub webhooks directly, and what are the workarounds?","a":"<p>GitHub must send an HTTP request to a URL it can reach, and a laptop behind NAT has no public address. Workarounds are hosting Jenkins on a public or VPN-reachable server, using a tunnel or relay for development, using a reverse proxy with a public endpoint, or falling back to <code>pollSCM</code>, which only needs outbound access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0043","card":"q-jenkins-009-requirements-and-where-to-host","sec":"jenkins-s010","q":"What do you need to size for a production Jenkins controller, and why not run it on a very small instance?","a":"<p>Jenkins is a Java application, so heap memory, disk for <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> and I/O matter most, along with the number of jobs, plugins and concurrent builds. Builds should run on agents, so the controller mostly needs memory and fast storage. A tiny instance is acceptable for learning but swaps or hits out-of-memory errors with many jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0044","card":"q-jenkins-009-requirements-and-where-to-host","sec":"jenkins-s010","q":"Which Java requirement matters when installing Jenkins, and why do install scripts need updating over time?","a":"<p>Jenkins runs on the JVM, so the installed Java must be a version the Jenkins release supports. Older releases ran on Java 8 or 11. Since Jenkins 2.463 (2024) Java 17 or newer is required. Pin versions and read the upgrade guide before upgrading Jenkins or the OS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0045","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"When Jenkins runs on EC2, why should the security group not allow SSH or the Jenkins port from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>?","a":"<p>Open ports invite brute-force and exploit attempts against a system that holds deployment credentials. Limit SSH to your own IP, a bastion or SSM Session Manager, and put Jenkins behind HTTPS and a reverse proxy or load balancer. Public access is only needed for the endpoints that must be reachable, such as the webhook path from GitHub's address ranges.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0046","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"Why attach an Elastic IP to the Jenkins EC2 instance?","a":"<p>A normal public IP changes when the instance is stopped and started, which breaks DNS records, the Jenkins URL setting and GitHub webhooks. An Elastic IP stays fixed. A load balancer or DNS name in front of the instance serves the same purpose.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0047","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"How should a Jenkins job running on EC2 get permission to call AWS services such as S3 or ECR?","a":"<p>Attach an IAM instance profile (role) with least privilege to the instance so the SDK or CLI receives temporary credentials. Do not store long-lived access keys in Jenkins when a role is available. For agents on Kubernetes use IAM roles for service accounts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0048","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"Why must a downloaded SSH key file be set to <code>chmod 600</code> before using <code>ssh -i</code>?","a":"<p>The SSH client refuses private keys that are readable by other users (\"UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE\"). <code>600</code> means only the owner can read and write, which protects the key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0049","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"Why put NGINX in front of Jenkins, and what does it add?","a":"<p>NGINX is a reverse proxy. It can terminate TLS, serve on ports 80 and 443 without running Jenkins as root, add compression, rate limits and access logs, and hide the internal port 8080. Jenkins also needs correct proxy headers and a configured Jenkins URL, otherwise it shows a reverse proxy misconfiguration warning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0050","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"In this NGINX site configuration, what does <code>proxy_pass</code> do, and why are the <code>proxy_set_header</code> lines needed?","a":"<p><code>proxy_pass</code> forwards each request to Jenkins on port 8080. The headers pass the original host name and client IP to Jenkins, which otherwise would see every request as coming from NGINX on localhost. That matters for links, redirects and audit logs. For HTTPS you also set <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>location / {\n    proxy_pass http://localhost:8080;\n    proxy_set_header Host \\$host;\n    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP \\$remote_addr;\n    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For \\$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0051","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"In an install script that writes the NGINX config with <code>cat &lt;&lt;EOF</code>, why is <code>$host</code> written as <code>\\$host</code>?","a":"<p>An unquoted heredoc delimiter lets the shell expand variables while writing the file, so <code>$host</code> would be replaced by an empty string. Escaping it as <code>\\$host</code> writes the literal text that NGINX should evaluate. Quoting the delimiter (<code>&lt;&lt;'EOF'</code>) is the other way to stop expansion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0052","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"In this install script, why does it add the Jenkins repository and key instead of installing Jenkins from the default Ubuntu repository?","a":"<p>The default Ubuntu repositories have no current Jenkins package. The Jenkins project repository provides it, and the key lets apt verify package signatures. <code>debian-stable</code> is the LTS line, while <code>debian</code> follows weekly releases. <code>apt-key</code> is deprecated on newer Debian and Ubuntu versions; there you store the key in a keyring file and reference it with <code>signed-by</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>wget -q -O - https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io.key | apt-key add -</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0053","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"Why is printing the content of <code>initialAdminPassword</code> in a script a security concern, and what should you do instead?","a":"<p>The password ends up in terminal history or logs. The file is meant for one-time setup, so change it right away. Better is to create the admin user through JCasC or Groovy init scripts with a secret from a secret store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0054","card":"q-jenkins-012-your-first-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s013","q":"In a Declarative Pipeline, what happens if <code>agent</code> is missing at the top level and not defined in each stage?","a":"<p>The pipeline fails validation, because a top-level <code>agent</code> section is required (use <code>agent none</code> and put an <code>agent</code> in each stage if you want per-stage agents). <code>agent any</code> allocates one executor on any node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0055","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"What are the key differences between Declarative and Scripted Pipeline syntax?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Declarative</strong>: opinionated and structured (<code>pipeline { ... }</code>), easier to read, with built-in <code>post</code>, <code>when</code> and <code>options</code>, validated up front. Preferred for most cases.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scripted</strong>: full Groovy (<code>node { ... }</code>), imperative, maximum flexibility but more complex and error-prone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can drop into scripted logic inside declarative with a <code>script {}</code> block.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Declarative\npipeline { agent any; stages { stage(&#x27;B&#x27;){ steps { sh &#x27;make&#x27; } } } }\n// Scripted\nnode { stage(&#x27;B&#x27;) { sh &#x27;make&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0056","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"Explain the core sections of a Declarative Pipeline.","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>pipeline</code>: the root block.</li>\n  <li><code>agent</code>: where it runs. <code>any</code> (any available executor), <code>label '...'</code> (a specific agent for an OS or tool), <code>docker</code> (inside a container for consistent environments), <code>none</code> (defer agent choice to individual stages).</li>\n  <li><code>stages</code>: container for one or more <code>stage</code>s (at least one required), such as Build, Test, Deploy.</li>\n  <li><code>stage</code>: a named phase.</li>\n  <li><code>steps</code>: the commands that do the work inside a stage (<code>sh</code>, <code>echo</code>); several commands are allowed per step block.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0057","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"In Declarative Pipeline, when would you set <code>agent none</code> at the top and define an <code>agent</code> per stage?","a":"<p>Use it when stages need different environments (for example build on Linux and test on Windows), or when a stage such as an approval should not occupy an executor while waiting. Each stage then declares its own <code>agent</code>, and the pipeline allocates executors only for the time they are needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0058","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"Which sections are required in a Declarative Pipeline, and how does its structure differ from Scripted Pipeline?","a":"<p>A Declarative Pipeline must be wrapped in a <code>pipeline { }</code> block and needs an <code>agent</code>, <code>stages</code> (with at least one <code>stage</code>) and <code>steps</code> inside each stage. Optional directives include <code>environment</code>, <code>options</code>, <code>parameters</code>, <code>tools</code>, <code>triggers</code>, <code>when</code> and <code>post</code>. Scripted Pipeline instead starts with <code>node { }</code> and is plain imperative Groovy with no fixed sections. Declarative trades some flexibility for a predictable, validated structure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0059","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"In a Declarative Pipeline, what do the <code>options</code>, <code>tools</code> and <code>triggers</code> directives each configure?","a":"<p><code>options</code> sets pipeline-wide behaviour such as <code>timeout</code>, <code>retry</code>, <code>disableConcurrentBuilds</code> and <code>buildDiscarder</code>. <code>tools</code> makes configured tool installations (for example a JDK or Maven) available on the <code>PATH</code>. <code>triggers</code> defines what starts the build automatically, such as <code>cron</code>, <code>pollSCM</code> or a webhook trigger.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0060","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"What does it mean that Declarative Pipeline is validated up front, and what does the user notice?","a":"<p>Jenkins parses the whole structure before running the first step, so a missing section or a misspelled directive fails the build immediately with a clear error. In Scripted Pipeline, errors in Groovy code show up only when execution reaches that line, possibly after a long build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0061","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"When would a team choose Scripted Pipeline instead of Declarative today?","a":"<p>Rarely: when the flow needs complex dynamic logic that is hard to express with directives, for example generating stages in loops from data. Even then, many teams keep Declarative for structure and use a <code>script {}</code> block or shared library for the dynamic part.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0062","card":"q-jenkins-014-a-multi-stage-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s015","q":"In a Jenkins Declarative Pipeline with Build, Test, Deploy and Release stages, what happens to later stages when the Test stage fails?","a":"<p>Jenkins stops the pipeline at the failing stage, marks the build failed and skips the later stages, so a broken build is never deployed. Stages that must always run belong in <code>post</code>. If a failure should not stop the pipeline, use <code>catchError</code> or <code>unstable</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0063","card":"q-jenkins-014-a-multi-stage-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s015","q":"What is the benefit of splitting a pipeline into several stages instead of one long <code>steps</code> block?","a":"<p>Stages give a visual view with per-stage timing and logs, show where a failure happened, allow <code>when</code> conditions, <code>post</code> handling and per-stage agents or environment, and enable restart from a stage. One long block gives none of that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0064","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"What is the Pipeline Snippet Generator used for, and why is it better than copying code from blog posts?","a":"<p>It builds correct step syntax for the plugins and versions installed on your own Jenkins, with every available option as a form field. Examples from the internet may use steps or parameters that your plugin version does not have.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0065","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"In this <code>archiveArtifacts</code> step, what do <code>allowEmptyArchive</code>, <code>onlyIfSuccessful</code> and <code>fingerprint</code> do?","a":"<p><code>allowEmptyArchive: true</code> stops the build from failing when no file matches the pattern. <code>onlyIfSuccessful: true</code> archives only when the build so far is successful. <code>fingerprint: true</code> records an MD5 fingerprint of each archived file, so Jenkins can trace which builds produced and used that file.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>archiveArtifacts artifacts: &#x27;*.txt&#x27;, allowEmptyArchive: true, onlyIfSuccessful: true, fingerprint: true</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0066","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"What is the Declarative Directive Generator, and how does it differ from the Snippet Generator?","a":"<p>The Directive Generator creates declarative sections such as <code>parameters</code>, <code>tools</code>, <code>options</code> and <code>when</code>. The Snippet Generator creates individual steps used inside <code>steps</code>. Directives shape the pipeline structure, steps do the work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0067","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"Why does Jenkins archive build output instead of leaving it in the workspace?","a":"<p>Workspaces are temporary and get overwritten or cleaned, and they sit on one agent. Archiving copies chosen files to the controller (or an artifact store) tied to the specific build, so they survive workspace cleanup and can be downloaded or reused later. Large artifacts are better pushed to an artifact repository.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0068","card":"q-jenkins-016-environment-variables-and-build-variable","sec":"jenkins-s017","q":"How do you define environment variables globally and at stage level?","a":"<p>Use an <code>environment</code> block at pipeline level (all stages) or inside a stage (that stage only). Reference with <code>${VAR}</code> or <code>env.VAR</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Name variables in capitals (<code>MAX_SIZE</code>) to tell them apart from lowercase keywords.</li>\n  <li>In strings prefix with <code>$</code> (<code>$VAR</code> or <code>${VAR}</code>), and stay consistent in the style you pick.</li>\n  <li>A stage-level definition overrides the global one for that stage only.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    environment {\n        MAX_SIZE = &#x27;10&#x27;\n        MIN_SIZE = &#x27;1&#x27;\n    }\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Default Scale&#x27;) {\n            steps { echo &quot;Default: Max size is ${MAX_SIZE}, Min size is ${MIN_SIZE}&quot; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Scale by 10X&#x27;) {\n            environment {\n                MAX_SIZE = &#x27;100&#x27;\n                MIN_SIZE = &#x27;10&#x27;\n            }\n            steps { echo &quot;Scaled: Max size is ${MAX_SIZE}, Min size is ${MIN_SIZE}&quot; }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The first stage prints 10 and 1, the second prints 100 and 10. The Pipeline Steps view shows each step's output clearly.</p>\n<p><strong>Current build variables</strong> are properties of the <code>currentBuild</code> object, accessed as <code>currentBuild.PROPERTY</code> (case-sensitive CamelCase): <code>currentBuild.startTime</code>, <code>currentBuild.duration</code>, <code>currentBuild.currentResult</code>. Use them for decisions based on build state, for example <code>echo \"Build duration: ${currentBuild.duration}\"</code>. The full list of default environment and <code>currentBuild</code> variables is under Pipeline Syntax, Global Variables Reference.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0069","card":"q-jenkins-016-environment-variables-and-build-variable","sec":"jenkins-s017","q":"In a Declarative Pipeline, how does <code>environment</code> at the stage level interact with one at pipeline level for the same variable name?","a":"<p>The stage value overrides the pipeline value only inside that stage. Other stages still see the pipeline-level value. Values are strings.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>environment {\n    MAX_SIZE = &#x27;10&#x27;\n    MIN_SIZE = &#x27;1&#x27;\n}\n...\nstage(&#x27;Scale by 10X&#x27;) {\n    environment {\n        MAX_SIZE = &#x27;100&#x27;\n        MIN_SIZE = &#x27;10&#x27;\n    }\n    ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0070","card":"q-jenkins-016-environment-variables-and-build-variable","sec":"jenkins-s017","q":"How do you pass a credential to a shell step as an environment variable in a Declarative Pipeline without printing it?","a":"<p>Use the <code>credentials()</code> helper in an <code>environment</code> block, for example <code>TOKEN = credentials('my-token-id')</code>. Jenkins injects the secret and masks it in console output. Single-quote the shell command (<code>sh 'curl -H \"Authorization: $TOKEN\" ...'</code>) so the shell reads the variable instead of Groovy putting the secret into the command text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0071","card":"q-jenkins-016-environment-variables-and-build-variable","sec":"jenkins-s017","q":"What is the difference between <code>env.VAR</code>, <code>params.VAR</code> and <code>currentBuild.PROPERTY</code> in a Jenkinsfile?","a":"<p><code>env</code> holds environment variables (including built-ins such as <code>BUILD_NUMBER</code> and <code>JOB_NAME</code>). <code>params</code> holds the build parameters chosen at start. <code>currentBuild</code> is an object describing the running build, such as <code>currentResult</code>, <code>duration</code> or <code>displayName</code>, and some of its fields can be set (for example <code>currentBuild.description</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0072","card":"q-jenkins-017-the-post-section","sec":"jenkins-s018","q":"What is the <code>post</code> section, and which conditions can it evaluate?","a":"<p><code>post</code> runs after a stage or the whole pipeline based on outcome: <code>always</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>failure</code>, <code>unstable</code>, <code>changed</code> (result differs from the last run), <code>aborted</code>, <code>fixed</code>, <code>regression</code>. It suits notifications and cleanup.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post {\n  always  { cleanWs() }\n  success { echo &#x27;OK&#x27; }\n  failure { mail to: &#x27;team@x.com&#x27;, subject: &quot;Failed: ${env.JOB_NAME}&quot;, body: &quot;${env.BUILD_URL}&quot; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0073","card":"q-jenkins-017-the-post-section","sec":"jenkins-s018","q":"Where can a <code>post</code> section be placed in a Declarative Pipeline, and why would you use a stage-level one?","a":"<p>It can be at the pipeline level or inside a stage. A stage-level <code>post</code> runs right after that stage finishes, so you can clean up or publish test results for that stage even when later stages are skipped.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0074","card":"q-jenkins-017-the-post-section","sec":"jenkins-s018","q":"Which conditions can a Declarative <code>post</code> section use, and in what order do they run?","a":"<p>The conditions are <code>always</code>, <code>changed</code>, <code>fixed</code>, <code>regression</code>, <code>aborted</code>, <code>failure</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>unstable</code>, <code>unsuccessful</code> and <code>cleanup</code>. Jenkins evaluates them in that order, so <code>always</code> runs first. <code>cleanup</code> runs last, after every other condition, regardless of the build result, which makes it the safe place for final cleanup such as removing a workspace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0075","card":"q-jenkins-017-the-post-section","sec":"jenkins-s018","q":"What is the difference between the <code>changed</code> and <code>fixed</code> conditions in <code>post</code>?","a":"<p><code>changed</code> runs when the result differs from the previous run (for example success to failure or failure to success). <code>fixed</code> runs only when the current run is successful and the previous one failed or was unstable. <code>fixed</code> is good for \"build is green again\" notifications.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0076","card":"q-jenkins-017-the-post-section","sec":"jenkins-s018","q":"In this <code>post</code> block, why is <code>cleanWs()</code> placed in <code>always</code>, and what is the risk of putting it in <code>success</code> only?","a":"<p><code>always</code> runs regardless of the result, so the workspace is cleaned after failures and aborts as well. Under <code>success</code> failed builds would leave workspaces behind and fill agent disks.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post {\n  always  { cleanWs() }\n  success { echo &#x27;OK&#x27; }\n  ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0077","card":"q-jenkins-017-the-post-section","sec":"jenkins-s018","q":"How can you publish JUnit test results even when the tests fail the build?","a":"<p>Put the <code>junit</code> step in a <code>post { always { junit 'reports/*.xml' } }</code> block. The <code>always</code> condition runs after a failed test step, so the failure details still show in Jenkins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0078","card":"q-jenkins-018-the-script-block","sec":"jenkins-s019","q":"What is the <code>script</code> block, and when should you use it?","a":"<p><code>script { }</code> embeds arbitrary Scripted (Groovy) code inside a declarative stage, for loops, complex conditionals or variable manipulation that declarative syntax cannot express. Use it sparingly; prefer shared-library steps for anything reusable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps {\n  script {\n    def services = [&#x27;api&#x27;, &#x27;web&#x27;, &#x27;worker&#x27;]\n    services.each { s -&gt; sh &quot;docker build -t ${s} ./${s}&quot; }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0079","card":"q-jenkins-018-the-script-block","sec":"jenkins-s019","q":"Why should a <code>script {}</code> block in a Declarative Pipeline be kept small?","a":"<p>It bypasses the structure and validation of Declarative syntax, runs under the Groovy CPS interpreter, and is harder to test and read. Large blocks belong in a shared library or in shell or Python scripts in the repository.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0080","card":"q-jenkins-018-the-script-block","sec":"jenkins-s019","q":"In this snippet, why is <code>script {}</code> needed to run the loop, and what does it show about Declarative Pipeline limits?","a":"<p>Declarative <code>steps</code> accepts only steps, not variable definitions or loops. <code>script {}</code> allows <code>def</code>, collections and iteration. A loop that creates parallel stages dynamically is the same kind of case.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps {\n  script {\n    def services = [&#x27;api&#x27;, &#x27;web&#x27;, &#x27;worker&#x27;]\n    services.each { s -&gt; sh &quot;docker build -t ${s} ./${s}&quot; }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0081","card":"q-jenkins-019-parallel-stages-and-matrix","sec":"jenkins-s020","q":"How do you run stages in parallel?","a":"<p>Wrap stages in a <code>parallel</code> block so they run concurrently on available executors or agents, which cuts wall-clock time for independent work.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Tests&#x27;) {\n  parallel {\n    stage(&#x27;Unit&#x27;)        { steps { sh &#x27;make unit&#x27; } }\n    stage(&#x27;Integration&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;make integration&#x27; } }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0082","card":"q-jenkins-019-parallel-stages-and-matrix","sec":"jenkins-s020","q":"What is the <code>matrix</code> directive?","a":"<p>It runs a stage's steps across combinations of <code>axes</code> (for example OS by JDK version) in parallel without repeating stage code, ideal for cross-platform or multi-version testing. <code>excludes</code> skips invalid combinations.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>matrix {\n  axes {\n    axis { name &#x27;OS&#x27;;  values &#x27;linux&#x27;, &#x27;windows&#x27; }\n    axis { name &#x27;JDK&#x27;; values &#x27;11&#x27;, &#x27;17&#x27; }\n  }\n  stages { stage(&#x27;Test&#x27;) { steps { echo &quot;Test on ${OS} JDK${JDK}&quot; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0083","card":"q-jenkins-019-parallel-stages-and-matrix","sec":"jenkins-s020","q":"What does <code>failFast true</code> do in a <code>parallel</code> block, and when would you not want it?","a":"<p>With <code>failFast true</code> Jenkins aborts the other parallel branches as soon as one fails, which saves time and resources. Do not use it when you want complete test results from all branches, for example separate browser suites where each failure is useful information.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0084","card":"q-jenkins-019-parallel-stages-and-matrix","sec":"jenkins-s020","q":"What are the risks of running stages in parallel on the same agent or workspace?","a":"<p>Steps can overwrite each other's files, compete for CPU, memory and ports, and make tests flaky. Give each branch its own agent or directory, use unique ports, and check that executors are available, because without free executors the branches queue and nothing runs concurrently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0085","card":"q-jenkins-019-parallel-stages-and-matrix","sec":"jenkins-s020","q":"When would you use <code>matrix</code> rather than writing <code>parallel</code> branches by hand, and what does <code>excludes</code> do?","a":"<p>Use <code>matrix</code> when the same stage logic runs over combinations of values (OS, JDK, browser). It avoids copy-paste and automatically builds the cross product. <code>excludes</code> removes combinations that make no sense, such as a browser that is not available on one OS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0086","card":"q-jenkins-020-timeout-retry-catcherror-and-platform-st","sec":"jenkins-s021","q":"How do <code>timeout</code>, <code>retry</code> and <code>catchError</code> work?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>timeout</code>: abort a block that exceeds a duration.</li>\n  <li><code>retry(n)</code>: re-run a block up to n times on failure.</li>\n  <li><code>catchError</code>: continue the pipeline but set the build result (for example UNSTABLE) instead of failing hard.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps {\n  retry(3) { timeout(time: 5, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./flaky.sh&#x27; } }\n  catchError(buildResult: &#x27;UNSTABLE&#x27;, stageResult: &#x27;FAILURE&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./optional.sh&#x27; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0087","card":"q-jenkins-020-timeout-retry-catcherror-and-platform-st","sec":"jenkins-s021","q":"What is the difference between <code>sh</code>, <code>bat</code> and <code>powershell</code> steps?","a":"<p><code>sh</code> runs shell commands on Unix, Linux and macOS agents; <code>bat</code> runs Windows batch and <code>powershell</code> runs PowerShell on Windows agents. Pick by agent OS (target it with labels): <code>sh</code> will not work on a Windows agent and vice versa.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    sh &#x27;mvn -B clean package&#x27;          // Linux agent\n    // bat &#x27;mvn -B clean package&#x27;      // Windows agent\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0088","card":"q-jenkins-020-timeout-retry-catcherror-and-platform-st","sec":"jenkins-s021","q":"In this snippet, what is the difference between <code>buildResult</code> and <code>stageResult</code> in <code>catchError</code>?","a":"<p><code>stageResult</code> sets the result of the stage that contains the block (here shown as failed in the stage view). <code>buildResult</code> sets the overall build result (here UNSTABLE, not FAILURE). The pipeline continues with later stages.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>catchError(buildResult: &#x27;UNSTABLE&#x27;, stageResult: &#x27;FAILURE&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./optional.sh&#x27; }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0089","card":"q-jenkins-020-timeout-retry-catcherror-and-platform-st","sec":"jenkins-s021","q":"In this snippet, what does the nesting order of <code>retry(3)</code> and <code>timeout</code> mean?","a":"<p>The timeout applies to each attempt separately, and a timed-out attempt counts as a failure that <code>retry</code> can repeat (so the worst case is 15 minutes). With <code>timeout</code> outside, the 5 minutes cover all attempts together. Retrying is a workaround for flaky steps and should not hide real defects.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>retry(3) { timeout(time: 5, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./flaky.sh&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0090","card":"q-jenkins-020-timeout-retry-catcherror-and-platform-st","sec":"jenkins-s021","q":"Why is <code>retry</code> risky for non-idempotent steps such as a deploy or a payment call?","a":"<p>A retry runs the whole block again, so a step that partly succeeded can be repeated (duplicate records, partial deployments). Retry only idempotent operations, or put the retry around the safe part (for example a network download).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0091","card":"q-jenkins-020-timeout-retry-catcherror-and-platform-st","sec":"jenkins-s021","q":"How would you run the same Jenkinsfile build step on Linux and Windows agents?","a":"<p>Use labels to choose the agent by OS. Since <code>sh</code> and <code>bat</code> are OS-specific, either branch with a condition (for example <code>isUnix()</code>) or use cross-platform commands, such as running <code>mvn</code> through the same tool call in a small wrapper.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0092","card":"q-jenkins-021-replay-and-restart-from-a-stage","sec":"jenkins-s022","q":"How do you replay or restart a build from a failed stage?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Replay</strong> re-runs a build with an editable copy of the pipeline script (good for quick experiments).</li>\n  <li><strong>Restart from Stage</strong> (declarative pipelines, with <code>preserveStashes</code>) restarts a completed run from a chosen stage, reusing earlier stashed artifacts so earlier successful stages are not repeated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are available from the build page in Blue Ocean or the classic UI.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { preserveStashes(buildCount: 5) }   // enables reliable restart-from-stage</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0093","card":"q-jenkins-021-replay-and-restart-from-a-stage","sec":"jenkins-s022","q":"How is Replay different from editing the Jenkinsfile and committing, and what is the catch?","a":"<p>Replay runs a modified copy of the script for that one build only. It is not saved to source control, so a good fix still has to be committed. It is useful for debugging pipeline code and shared library changes quickly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0094","card":"q-jenkins-021-replay-and-restart-from-a-stage","sec":"jenkins-s022","q":"What are the conditions for Restart from Stage to work in Jenkins?","a":"<p>It is available for Declarative Pipelines that already finished (failed or succeeded), and it reuses the original SCM revision and parameters. Files that earlier stages passed through <code>stash</code> are kept only if <code>preserveStashes</code> is set. The workspace is not guaranteed to be the same, so do not rely on leftover files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0095","card":"q-jenkins-022-pipeline-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s023","q":"Why is a <code>password</code> parameter a weak way to handle real secrets in Jenkins?","a":"<p>The value is still passed in through the job and may be visible in build records or logs when echoed or interpolated, and anyone with the right permissions can see how it was used. Store secrets as Jenkins credentials and use <code>credentials()</code> or <code>withCredentials</code> so the value is masked and access-controlled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0096","card":"q-jenkins-022-pipeline-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s023","q":"What is the difference between reading a parameter as <code>params.NAME</code> and as <code>NAME</code> or <code>env.NAME</code>?","a":"<p>Jenkins exposes parameters both as <code>params.NAME</code> and as environment variables. <code>params.NAME</code> keeps the parameter type (for example a real boolean for <code>booleanParam</code>). In <code>env.NAME</code> or in shell variables everything is a string, so <code>'false'</code> is truthy if tested in Groovy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0097","card":"q-jenkins-022-pipeline-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s023","q":"How do you decide between a <code>string</code> parameter and a <code>choice</code> parameter for an environment name?","a":"<p>Use <code>choice</code> when the valid values are a fixed list, because it prevents typos and unsafe input and documents the options. Use <code>string</code> for free values such as a version or branch. Never feed an unvalidated string into a shell command.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0098","card":"q-jenkins-022-pipeline-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s023","q":"In Jenkins, where do you define parameters so that they live in code and not in the job configuration?","a":"<p>Put a <code>parameters {}</code> block in the Jenkinsfile (or use the <code>properties([parameters([...])])</code> step in Scripted Pipeline). The job's parameter definitions are then updated from the Jenkinsfile when the pipeline runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0099","card":"q-jenkins-023-first-build-and-build-with-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s024","q":"How do you configure parameterised builds (String, Choice, Boolean, Git Parameter)?","a":"<p>Declare a <code>parameters</code> block; values are supplied at build time and read via <code>params.NAME</code>. The Git Parameter plugin adds a <code>gitParameter</code> type for choosing branches or tags.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>parameters {\n  string(name: &#x27;VERSION&#x27;, defaultValue: &#x27;1.0.0&#x27;, description: &#x27;Release version&#x27;)\n  choice(name: &#x27;ENVIRONMENT&#x27;, choices: [&#x27;dev&#x27;,&#x27;staging&#x27;,&#x27;prod&#x27;], description: &#x27;Target&#x27;)\n  booleanParam(name: &#x27;RUN_TESTS&#x27;, defaultValue: true, description: &#x27;Run tests?&#x27;)\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0100","card":"q-jenkins-023-first-build-and-build-with-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s024","q":"Why does a new Jenkinsfile with a <code>parameters</code> block not show Build with Parameters before the first build, and how can you avoid that problem?","a":"<p>With parameters defined in the Jenkinsfile, Jenkins learns about them only after a run reads the script. The first run therefore happens without them. Avoid it by running one initial build, defining parameters in the job UI, or generating the job definition with JCasC and Job DSL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0101","card":"q-jenkins-024-conditional-stages-with-when","sec":"jenkins-s025","q":"How do you run a stage conditionally with <code>when</code>?","a":"<p><code>when</code> decides whether a stage runs. If it evaluates to true the stage runs, otherwise it is skipped. Conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>branch</code>: only for given Git branches (for example <code>main</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>environment</code>: an environment variable has a given value.</li>\n  <li><code>expression</code>: a Groovy expression using parameters or variables (for example <code>params.ENV != 'prod'</code>).</li>\n  <li>Others include <code>changeRequest</code> and <code>tag</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Deploy Prod&#x27;) {\n  when { branch &#x27;main&#x27;; expression { params.ENVIRONMENT == &#x27;prod&#x27; } }\n  steps { echo &#x27;Deploying to production&#x27; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0102","card":"q-jenkins-024-conditional-stages-with-when","sec":"jenkins-s025","q":"What does <code>beforeAgent true</code> do in a <code>when</code> block, and why is it useful?","a":"<p>By default <code>when</code> is evaluated after the stage's agent is allocated. With <code>beforeAgent true</code> Jenkins evaluates the condition first, so skipped stages do not wait for or occupy an agent. This saves resources, especially for Docker or Kubernetes agents that are expensive to start.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0103","card":"q-jenkins-024-conditional-stages-with-when","sec":"jenkins-s025","q":"In this <code>when</code> block, are the two conditions combined with AND or OR, and how would you change that?","a":"<p>Multiple conditions in <code>when</code> are combined with AND, so both must be true. Use <code>anyOf { ... }</code> for OR, <code>allOf { ... }</code> to be explicit, and <code>not { ... }</code> to negate.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>when { branch &#x27;main&#x27;; expression { params.ENVIRONMENT == &#x27;prod&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0104","card":"q-jenkins-024-conditional-stages-with-when","sec":"jenkins-s025","q":"What is the difference between a <code>when</code> condition and a Groovy <code>if</code> inside a <code>script</code> block for skipping work?","a":"<p><code>when</code> is part of the declarative model: the stage is shown as skipped in the UI, and conditions such as <code>beforeAgent</code> and <code>beforeInput</code> work with it. An <code>if</code> inside <code>script</code> just skips code, so the stage shows as successful with no work done. Prefer <code>when</code> for stage-level decisions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0105","card":"q-jenkins-024-conditional-stages-with-when","sec":"jenkins-s025","q":"Why does the <code>branch</code> condition in <code>when</code> only work in a Multibranch Pipeline?","a":"<p><code>branch</code> compares against the branch name that Jenkins sets for a multibranch job. In a plain Pipeline job there is no branch context, so the condition does not match as you expect. Use <code>expression</code> with <code>env.GIT_BRANCH</code> there.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0106","card":"q-jenkins-024-conditional-stages-with-when","sec":"jenkins-s025","q":"In a Jenkins Scripted or Declarative Pipeline, what does <code>withEnv</code> do, and how does it differ from the <code>environment</code> directive?","a":"<p><code>withEnv</code> sets environment variables only for the steps inside its block, as a list of <code>NAME=value</code> strings, and can be used anywhere a step can, including Scripted Pipelines and <code>script</code> blocks. The <code>environment</code> directive is declarative: it applies to the whole pipeline or one stage and is evaluated when the stage starts. Use <code>withEnv</code> when the value is computed at run time or needed for a few steps only.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>node {\n    withEnv([&#x27;DEPLOY_ENV=staging&#x27;]) {\n        sh &#x27;echo $DEPLOY_ENV&#x27;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0107","card":"q-jenkins-025-manual-approvals-with-input","sec":"jenkins-s026","q":"How do you require a manual approval before production?","a":"<p>The <code>input</code> step pauses the pipeline for a human to Proceed or Abort, with a custom message to explain the action. Wrap it in <code>timeout</code> so it cannot hang forever.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Approve&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    timeout(time: 15, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) {\n      input message: &#x27;Deploy to production?&#x27;, ok: &#x27;Deploy&#x27;\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The paused stage shows a confirmation dialog when you hover over it in the pipeline view.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0108","card":"q-jenkins-025-manual-approvals-with-input","sec":"jenkins-s026","q":"What is the problem with putting an <code>input</code> step inside a stage that has its own agent, and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>The pipeline keeps the executor allocated while waiting for a human, which can block other builds for hours. Put the approval in a stage with no agent (use <code>agent none</code> at pipeline level) or use the stage-level <code>input</code> directive, which pauses before the stage's <code>agent</code> is allocated, so no executor is held while waiting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0109","card":"q-jenkins-025-manual-approvals-with-input","sec":"jenkins-s026","q":"How do you restrict who can approve an <code>input</code> step in Jenkins?","a":"<p>Use the <code>submitter</code> option to name the users or groups allowed to proceed, for example <code>input message: 'Deploy?', submitter: 'release-managers'</code>. Without it, any user with the right permission on the job can approve. Use <code>submitterParameter</code> to record who approved.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0110","card":"q-jenkins-025-manual-approvals-with-input","sec":"jenkins-s026","q":"Why should every <code>input</code> step be inside a <code>timeout</code>?","a":"<p>A pipeline waiting for input never finishes if nobody answers. It holds resources and the build stays in progress. A timeout aborts it after a set time, so the queue stays clean and approval requests do not stay open forever.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0111","card":"q-jenkins-025-manual-approvals-with-input","sec":"jenkins-s026","q":"Can an <code>input</code> step collect values from the approver, and how would you use it?","a":"<p>Yes. The <code>parameters</code> option of <code>input</code> prompts the user for values (for example a choice or text) and returns them to the pipeline, so the approver can select the target region or a release note at approval time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0112","card":"q-jenkins-026-conditional-deploy-example","sec":"jenkins-s027","q":"In Jenkins, how would you make production deployments require approval while dev and staging deploy automatically?","a":"<p>Use two stages with complementary <code>when</code> conditions. The production stage contains an <code>input</code> step, so only production runs wait for a human. Use the same deploy script for both and pass the environment as a parameter, to avoid divergence between paths. In real pipelines also add <code>timeout</code>, <code>beforeAgent true</code> and a restricted <code>submitter</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Deploy to Non-Production&#x27;) {\n    when { expression { params.ENVIRONMENT != &#x27;prod&#x27; } }\n    ...\n}\nstage(&#x27;Deploy to Production&#x27;) {\n    when { expression { params.ENVIRONMENT == &#x27;prod&#x27; } }\n    steps {\n        input message: &#x27;Confirm deployment to production?&#x27;, ok: &#x27;Deploy&#x27;\n        ...\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0113","card":"q-jenkins-027-parameterized-pipeline-exercise","sec":"jenkins-s028","q":"In this step, what is the injection risk from interpolating <code>params.CHANGELOG</code> into a shell command, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>With a double-quoted Groovy string, Jenkins puts the parameter text into the shell command before the shell runs. A single quote or <code>$(...)</code> in the value breaks out of the quoting and can run arbitrary commands. Use single quotes so the shell expands the value as an environment variable (<code>sh 'printf \"%s\\n\" \"$CHANGELOG\" &gt; \"$ENVIRONMENT.txt\"'</code>), since parameters are exported as environment variables. Better still, validate input and prefer <code>choice</code> parameters.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sh &quot;echo &#x27;${params.CHANGELOG}&#x27; &gt; ${params.ENVIRONMENT}.txt&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0114","card":"q-jenkins-027-parameterized-pipeline-exercise","sec":"jenkins-s028","q":"Why is it a mistake to echo a <code>password</code> parameter or secret like <code>echo \"Deploying with API key: ${params.API_KEY}\"</code>?","a":"<p>The value is written to the console log in plain text (masking applies only to registered credentials), and Groovy string interpolation of secrets is flagged by Jenkins as unsafe. Use credentials bound with <code>withCredentials</code>, and never print secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0115","card":"q-jenkins-028-jenkinsfile-and-pipeline-as-code","sec":"jenkins-s029","q":"What is a Jenkinsfile, and why store it in source control?","a":"<p>A <code>Jenkinsfile</code> is a text file defining the pipeline, committed alongside the code. Benefits: version control and code review, auditable history, reproducibility across branches and environments, self-documenting pipelines, and Multibranch pipelines that build every branch and PR automatically. It also supports a <strong>GitOps</strong> approach where the repository is the single source of truth for application code, tool configuration and CI changes, which can be reviewed and merged through automated processes. A Jenkinsfile can hold tools, options, triggers and more.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0116","card":"q-jenkins-028-jenkinsfile-and-pipeline-as-code","sec":"jenkins-s029","q":"How do you validate a Jenkinsfile for syntax errors before committing it?","a":"<p>Use the Declarative linter: the Jenkins CLI command <code>declarative-linter</code> or the HTTP endpoint <code>/pipeline-model-converter/validate</code>, or the editor plugin that calls them. Running a pull request build on a test controller is another safeguard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0117","card":"q-jenkins-028-jenkinsfile-and-pipeline-as-code","sec":"jenkins-s029","q":"Where should a Jenkinsfile be placed, and why is its name and location important?","a":"<p>By default in the repository root with the name <code>Jenkinsfile</code>, because Multibranch Pipelines and organization folders look for that file in each branch. Other paths can be configured, but standard names make scanning and tooling work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0118","card":"q-jenkins-028-jenkinsfile-and-pipeline-as-code","sec":"jenkins-s029","q":"Why does keeping pipeline definitions in the same repository as the application code help with changes that need both an application and a pipeline update?","a":"<p>One commit or pull request changes both, so they are reviewed, tested and rolled back together. Each branch builds with the pipeline that matches its code, so old release branches keep working with their own build steps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0119","card":"q-jenkins-029-pipeline-script-from-scm","sec":"jenkins-s030","q":"What is the difference between \"Pipeline script\" and \"Pipeline script from SCM\" in a Jenkins Pipeline job?","a":"<p>\"Pipeline script\" stores the script inside the Jenkins job configuration. \"Pipeline script from SCM\" tells Jenkins to fetch the Jenkinsfile from a repository at build time, so the pipeline is versioned and reviewed like code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0120","card":"q-jenkins-029-pipeline-script-from-scm","sec":"jenkins-s030","q":"What does the Branch Specifier do in a Jenkins Pipeline from SCM job, and what goes wrong if it names a branch that does not exist?","a":"<p>It selects which branch Jenkins checks out to read the Jenkinsfile and the code. A wrong name, for example <code>master</code> when the repository uses <code>main</code>, makes the checkout fail with an error such as \"couldn't find any revision to build\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0121","card":"q-jenkins-029-pipeline-script-from-scm","sec":"jenkins-s030","q":"What is the Lightweight Checkout option for Pipeline from SCM?","a":"<p>Jenkins fetches only the Jenkinsfile from the repository (when the SCM supports it) to start the pipeline, instead of a full checkout on the controller. That is faster and keeps repository data off the controller. The actual build still checks out the code on the agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0122","card":"q-jenkins-029-pipeline-script-from-scm","sec":"jenkins-s030","q":"Why does a pipeline that has a <code>git</code> step in a stage still work when the Jenkinsfile comes from SCM, and is that step redundant?","a":"<p>The SCM setting only loads the Jenkinsfile. The agent workspace gets the code through a checkout. In \"Pipeline script from SCM\" and Multibranch jobs, <code>checkout scm</code> reuses the same repository and revision configuration. An extra <code>git</code> step with a hard-coded branch can fetch a different revision than the one that triggered the build, so prefer <code>checkout scm</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0123","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"What are the ways to trigger a Jenkins job?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Webhooks</strong>: the SCM pushes an event and the build starts instantly (best).</li>\n  <li><strong>Poll SCM</strong>: Jenkins periodically checks the repo for changes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scheduled (cron)</strong>: build on a time schedule regardless of changes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Remote API or token</strong>: trigger over HTTP (<code>buildWithParameters</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Upstream</strong>: build after another project completes.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>triggers {\n  githubPush()                 // webhook\n  pollSCM(&#x27;H/5 * * * *&#x27;)       // poll every ~5 min\n  cron(&#x27;H 2 * * *&#x27;)            // nightly ~2am\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0124","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"What is the difference between <code>pollSCM</code> and <code>cron</code>?","a":"<p><code>pollSCM('schedule')</code> checks SCM on that schedule and builds only if there are changes. <code>cron('schedule')</code> builds unconditionally on the schedule (nightly jobs). Use <code>H</code> (hash) to spread load and avoid every job firing on the hour.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0125","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"How do GitHub or GitLab webhooks trigger builds instantly?","a":"<p>The SCM sends an HTTP POST to Jenkins (<code>/github-webhook/</code> or the GitLab hook URL) on push or PR events. Jenkins matches it to jobs watching that repo and builds immediately, which is faster and cheaper than polling.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0126","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"Why is a webhook preferred over <code>pollSCM</code> for starting builds?","a":"<p>Webhooks start a build within seconds of a push and cause no repeated requests. Polling checks on a schedule, which delays builds and puts load on the SCM server when there are many jobs. Polling is a fallback when Jenkins is not reachable from the SCM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0127","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"What does <code>H/5 * * * *</code> mean in Jenkins cron syntax, and why is <code>H</code> used instead of <code>0</code>?","a":"<p>It runs about every 5 minutes, at a minute offset chosen by hashing the job name. <code>H</code> spreads jobs over the interval so that they do not all start at the same minute, which prevents load spikes on the controller and the SCM. Syntax follows cron with fields minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0128","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"How do you make a nightly build that runs only if the code changed since the last build?","a":"<p>Use <code>pollSCM</code> with a nightly schedule, which checks for new commits and builds only when there are changes. <code>cron</code> always builds at the given time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0129","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"How would you trigger the same pipeline from another system, such as a ticketing tool or a script?","a":"<p>Call the job's remote build URL with an authenticated user API token, or use the Generic Webhook Trigger with a token. Restrict the user to the minimum permission (Job Build) and avoid putting the secret in URLs that get logged.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0130","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"What is a Multibranch Pipeline?","a":"<p>It scans a repo and creates and runs a pipeline for every branch and PR that contains a <code>Jenkinsfile</code>. New branches create new jobs and deleted branches remove them. Each branch builds with its own Jenkinsfile, so per-branch CI needs no manual job setup.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0131","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"What is the GitHub Organization Folder or GitLab Group plugin?","a":"<p>They scan an entire GitHub org or GitLab group and auto-create a Multibranch Pipeline for every repo containing a Jenkinsfile. New repos are picked up on the next scan or webhook.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0132","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"How do you build pull requests and report status back?","a":"<p>Use a Multibranch Pipeline with the GitHub or GitLab Branch Source: it discovers PRs and builds them, and Jenkins reports commit status (pending, success, failure) to the PR automatically (or with <code>githubNotify</code> or the GitLab status). Combine with branch protection to require the check before merge.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0133","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"What is a Jenkins Multibranch Pipeline, and why use it instead of one Pipeline job?","a":"<p>A Multibranch Pipeline scans a repository and automatically creates a job for every branch and pull request that contains a <code>Jenkinsfile</code>. Each branch builds with its own Jenkinsfile, so there is no manual job setup per branch and branch jobs appear and disappear with the repository. A single Pipeline job builds only one fixed branch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0134","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"What is an Organization Folder in Jenkins, and how does it find repositories?","a":"<p>An Organization Folder (GitHub Organization or GitLab Group plugins) scans a whole GitHub organization or GitLab group and creates a Multibranch Pipeline for each repository that has a <code>Jenkinsfile</code>. It discovers new repositories on the next scheduled scan or when a webhook event arrives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0135","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"In a Multibranch Pipeline, what happens to the job of a branch that is deleted in Git?","a":"<p>The next branch indexing detects that the branch is gone and marks the item as orphaned, then removes it according to the orphaned item strategy (for example keep for some days or builds). Branch jobs therefore follow the repository lifecycle without manual cleanup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0136","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"A Multibranch job does not build a branch you just pushed. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that the branch contains a Jenkinsfile at the configured path, that a scan or webhook ran (run \"Scan Repository Now\" to check), that branch discovery filters or behaviours do not exclude it, that credentials can read the repository, and the scan log for errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0137","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"How do you run production deploy steps only for the main branch and not for feature branches in a Multibranch Pipeline?","a":"<p>Use <code>when { branch 'main' }</code> on the deploy stage. Feature branches and pull requests still build and test but skip the deploy stage. Add an approval step if needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0138","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"What is the difference between building a pull request branch and building the merged result in Jenkins?","a":"<p>By default the PR build uses the head of the PR branch, which may be out of date compared with the target. With the \"merge with target\" behaviour Jenkins builds the result of the merge, which finds integration failures before the PR is merged. Both options exist with GitHub Branch Source.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0139","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"How do you trigger downstream jobs and pass parameters?","a":"<p>Use the <code>build</code> step with <code>parameters</code>. <code>wait</code> and <code>propagate</code> control blocking and result propagation.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>build job: &#x27;deploy-service&#x27;,\n  parameters: [string(name: &#x27;VERSION&#x27;, value: env.BUILD_NUMBER)],\n  wait: true, propagate: true</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0140","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"How do you trigger a build remotely with cURL and an API token?","a":"<p>Create a user API token, then POST to the job's <code>build</code> or <code>buildWithParameters</code> endpoint (with a crumb if CSRF protection is on). Trigger tokens can also be set per job.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>curl -X POST &quot;https://jenkins.example.com/job/my-job/buildWithParameters?VERSION=1.2.3&quot; \\\n  --user &quot;alice:11aabbccddeeff...&quot;   # user:apiToken</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0141","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"What is the Generic Webhook Trigger plugin?","a":"<p>It lets any system trigger a job through one webhook URL and extracts fields from the JSON or XML payload into variables with JSONPath. The variables can feed an optional filter that decides whether to build.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>triggers {\n  GenericTrigger(\n    genericVariables: [[key: &#x27;ref&#x27;, value: &#x27;$.ref&#x27;]],\n    token: &#x27;my-token&#x27;,\n    regexpFilterText: &#x27;$ref&#x27;, regexpFilterExpression: &#x27;refs/heads/main&#x27;\n  )\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0142","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"What do <code>wait</code> and <code>propagate</code> control in the <code>build</code> step, and what are the typical combinations?","a":"<p><code>wait: true</code> makes the upstream pipeline wait until the downstream job finishes. <code>propagate: true</code> makes the upstream result fail when the downstream fails. With <code>wait: false</code> the call is fire-and-forget and the upstream continues. With <code>wait: true, propagate: false</code> you wait but decide yourself what to do with the result (the step returns a build object).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>build job: &#x27;deploy-service&#x27;,\n  parameters: [string(name: &#x27;VERSION&#x27;, value: env.BUILD_NUMBER)],\n  wait: true, propagate: true</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0143","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"What are the drawbacks of chaining many jobs with the <code>build</code> step compared with one pipeline with stages?","a":"<p>The flow is split across several jobs, so tracing a release, passing artifacts and reading the full log is harder, and the jobs can drift. Use downstream jobs for separate ownership or different triggers, and keep related steps in one pipeline when possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0144","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"Why is the Generic Webhook Trigger useful when the event source is not GitHub or GitLab?","a":"<p>It exposes a configurable endpoint that accepts any JSON or XML payload and maps fields to variables with JSONPath. It can then filter on those values, for example build only when the <code>ref</code> equals the main branch. That connects tools without a dedicated plugin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0145","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"What is Git sparse checkout, and how do you speed up large checkouts?","a":"<p>Sparse checkout fetches only specified paths instead of the whole repo, which helps monorepos. Other options: shallow clone (<code>depth</code>), skipping tags, reference repos, and disabling unneeded submodules.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>checkout([$class: &#x27;GitSCM&#x27;,\n  branches: [[name: &#x27;*/main&#x27;]],\n  extensions: [\n    [$class: &#x27;CloneOption&#x27;, depth: 1, shallow: true, noTags: true],\n    [$class: &#x27;SparseCheckoutPaths&#x27;, sparseCheckoutPaths: [[path: &#x27;services/api/&#x27;]]]\n  ],\n  userRemoteConfigs: [[url: &#x27;https://github.com/org/monorepo.git&#x27;]]\n])</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0146","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"How do you handle Git submodules during checkout?","a":"<p>Enable the <code>SubmoduleOption</code> extension (recursive, with parent credentials) so submodules are initialized and updated.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>checkout([$class: &#x27;GitSCM&#x27;, branches: [[name: &#x27;*/main&#x27;]],\n  extensions: [[$class: &#x27;SubmoduleOption&#x27;, recursiveSubmodules: true, parentCredentials: true]],\n  userRemoteConfigs: [[url: &#x27;https://github.com/org/repo.git&#x27;]]])</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0147","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"How do you stop concurrent builds of the same branch?","a":"<p>Add <code>disableConcurrentBuilds</code>: a new build of the same job or branch queues until the current one finishes, avoiding races on shared resources and deploys. <code>abortPrevious: true</code> cancels the older run instead.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { disableConcurrentBuilds() }\n// or: options { disableConcurrentBuilds(abortPrevious: true) }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0148","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"What is the Quiet Period?","a":"<p>A configurable delay between a trigger firing and the build starting. It batches a burst of rapid commits into one build and avoids redundant runs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0149","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"What is the drawback of a shallow clone (<code>depth: 1</code>) in a Jenkins checkout?","a":"<p>The repository has no full history. Tools that need history break or give wrong results: <code>git describe</code>, changelog generation, merge-base calculations, version numbers from tags and blame-based analysis. Use a deeper clone or fetch tags when the build needs them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0150","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"In this submodule checkout, what does <code>parentCredentials: true</code> do?","a":"<p>It makes submodule updates use the same credentials as the parent repository, so private submodules can be fetched without configuring separate credentials. <code>recursiveSubmodules: true</code> also initializes submodules nested inside submodules.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>extensions: [[$class: &#x27;SubmoduleOption&#x27;, recursiveSubmodules: true, parentCredentials: true]],</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0151","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"What is the difference between <code>disableConcurrentBuilds()</code> and <code>disableConcurrentBuilds(abortPrevious: true)</code>?","a":"<p>The plain form queues the new build until the running one ends. With <code>abortPrevious: true</code> the running build is cancelled when a newer one starts. Aborting suits CI on pull requests where only the newest commit matters, while queuing suits deployments that must not be interrupted halfway.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0152","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"What problem does the Quiet Period solve, and what is its trade-off?","a":"<p>When several commits or events arrive in quick succession, Jenkins waits a short time before starting, so they are combined into one build. The trade-off is a small delay before every triggered build. The global default is 5 seconds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0153","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"Why keep build logic in scripts in the repository rather than writing long <code>sh</code> blocks in the Jenkinsfile?","a":"<p>Scripts can be run and tested locally, work with any CI system, are easier to lint and review, and keep the Jenkinsfile short. The Jenkinsfile then only orchestrates stages and the scripts do the work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0154","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"A Jenkins <code>sh './scripts/build.sh'</code> step fails with \"Permission denied\". What are the likely causes and fixes?","a":"<p>The script is not executable. Set the bit with <code>chmod +x</code> in the repository (<code>git update-index --chmod=+x</code>) or run <code>sh scripts/build.sh</code>. Other causes are a wrong shebang line, Windows line endings (CRLF) or a missing interpreter on the agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0155","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"What does the <code>dir('scripts') { ... }</code> step do, and how is it different from <code>cd</code> inside an <code>sh</code> step?","a":"<p><code>dir</code> changes the working directory for all pipeline steps inside its block and restores it afterwards. A <code>cd</code> inside one <code>sh</code> call affects only that shell invocation, because each <code>sh</code> step starts a new shell in the workspace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0156","card":"q-jenkins-035-prepare-the-repository-and-the-job","sec":"jenkins-s036","q":"What does the option \"GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling\" do in a Jenkins job?","a":"<p>It lets Jenkins react to push notifications from GitHub. When a webhook arrives for the repository, Jenkins runs an SCM poll for that job and starts a build if there is a change. In Pipeline code the equivalent is <code>githubPush()</code> in <code>triggers</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0157","card":"q-jenkins-035-prepare-the-repository-and-the-job","sec":"jenkins-s036","q":"Why does a new Pipeline-from-SCM job often need one manual build before the webhook trigger works?","a":"<p>Jenkins registers triggers and repository details only after the job has run once and read its configuration. Until then it does not know which repository the push notification belongs to. A first manual build fixes it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0158","card":"q-jenkins-036-create-the-github-webhook","sec":"jenkins-s037","q":"A GitHub push does not start your Jenkins build. How do you troubleshoot it?","a":"<p>In GitHub open the webhook's recent deliveries and check the response code. Then check that the payload URL ends with <code>/github-webhook/</code>, Jenkins is reachable from the internet (security group, proxy, TLS), the content type matches, the job has the GitHub trigger enabled and ran once, and the branch specifier matches the pushed branch. Finally read the Jenkins log for the hook.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0159","card":"q-jenkins-036-create-the-github-webhook","sec":"jenkins-s037","q":"What response codes in GitHub's webhook delivery list point to which kind of problem?","a":"<p>A timeout or connection error means GitHub cannot reach Jenkins (network or firewall). <code>403</code> often means Jenkins security or the proxy blocks the request, for example a missing crumb exemption or an authentication layer in front. <code>404</code> means the URL path is wrong. <code>200</code> means Jenkins received it, so the cause is job configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0160","card":"q-jenkins-036-create-the-github-webhook","sec":"jenkins-s037","q":"Why is it better to use a webhook secret and restrict the endpoint when Jenkins is public?","a":"<p>The webhook endpoint is open to anyone who can reach it, and a forged request could trigger builds. A shared secret lets the receiver verify that GitHub sent the payload (HMAC signature), and IP allowlisting limits who can call the endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0161","card":"q-jenkins-037-status-badge-in-readme","sec":"jenkins-s038","q":"What are the security considerations of the \"Unprotected\" link of the Embeddable Build Status plugin?","a":"<p>The unprotected badge link is readable without logging in, so anyone can see the job name and its pass or fail state, even if the job is private. Use it only for public information, or use the protected link variant with authentication for internal repositories.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0162","card":"q-jenkins-037-status-badge-in-readme","sec":"jenkins-s038","q":"Why does a build status badge help a team, and what does it not tell you?","a":"<p>It gives immediate visibility of whether the main branch builds. It does not tell you about test quality, coverage or deploy health, and a badge for the wrong branch or job can be misleading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0163","card":"q-jenkins-039-default-security-realms-and-authorizatio","sec":"jenkins-s040","q":"How do you integrate Jenkins with LDAP, Active Directory or SAML/Okta for SSO?","a":"<p>Set the Security Realm to LDAP, Active Directory, or a SAML/OIDC plugin. Jenkins delegates authentication to the identity provider, and group memberships flow in and map to authorization roles or the matrix. This centralizes identity and enables SSO.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0164","card":"q-jenkins-039-default-security-realms-and-authorizatio","sec":"jenkins-s040","q":"In Jenkins, why is the default \"Logged-in users can do anything\" authorization risky on a shared server?","a":"<p>Every authenticated user, including someone who only needs to view builds, can configure jobs, read credentials usage, install plugins and run arbitrary scripts on the controller. A compromised ordinary account is a full compromise. Use matrix or role-based authorization with least privilege.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0165","card":"q-jenkins-039-default-security-realms-and-authorizatio","sec":"jenkins-s040","q":"Should anonymous users be allowed read access on a Jenkins controller?","a":"<p>Usually not. Anonymous read exposes job names, logs and configuration details, which can contain internal hostnames or leaked secrets. Allow it only for deliberately public instances, and then limit it to what is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0166","card":"q-jenkins-039-default-security-realms-and-authorizatio","sec":"jenkins-s040","q":"Why does Jenkins separate the Security Realm from the Authorization Strategy, and what does this allow?","a":"<p>You can change how users log in (LDAP, Active Directory, SAML or OIDC single sign-on) without changing who may do what, and the reverse. For example you can keep an LDAP login and move from a global matrix to role-based permissions mapped to LDAP groups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0167","card":"q-jenkins-040-matrix-based-security","sec":"jenkins-s041","q":"What is the difference between matrix-based security and Role-Based Access Control (Role Strategy plugin)?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Matrix-based</strong>: a global grid mapping users and groups to permissions. Simple, but it does not scale because every user needs a row.</li>\n  <li><strong>Role Strategy (RBAC)</strong>: define reusable roles (global plus per-project or folder patterns) and assign users or groups to them; far more scalable for many users and teams.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Configure matrix security:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Manage Jenkins, Manage Users: create users (username, password, name, email).</li>\n  <li>Manage Jenkins, Configure Global Security: switch from \"Logged-in users can do anything\" to <strong>Matrix-based security</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Add your admin account and tick <strong>Administer</strong>. Add other users with specific permissions such as Overall Read (interact with the UI) and Job Build, Cancel, Read.</li>\n  <li>Save, then log in as the restricted user and confirm there is no management menu and only limited job actions.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Best practice: limit admin access to one or two users on a shared system and give everyone else role-based permissions. Always grant your own account Administer before saving, because locking yourself out is common (the Jenkins docs explain how to disable access control to recover).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0168","card":"q-jenkins-040-matrix-based-security","sec":"jenkins-s041","q":"You locked yourself out of Jenkins after enabling matrix-based security without granting yourself Administer. How do you recover?","a":"<p>Stop Jenkins, edit <code>config.xml</code> in <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> and set <code>&lt;useSecurity&gt;false&lt;/useSecurity&gt;</code> (or remove the authorization section), then restart. Log in, fix the security setup, and enable security again. Take a backup of <code>config.xml</code> first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0169","card":"q-jenkins-040-matrix-based-security","sec":"jenkins-s041","q":"Why assign permissions to groups instead of individual users in Jenkins matrix security?","a":"<p>Group assignments follow the identity provider, so users who join or leave a team get or lose access without editing Jenkins. Per-user rows cause drift, are hard to audit and do not scale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0170","card":"q-jenkins-040-matrix-based-security","sec":"jenkins-s041","q":"What does Overall Read permission give, and why does a restricted user still need it?","a":"<p>Overall Read lets a user see the Jenkins UI and basic items. Without it a user cannot even open the dashboard to reach the jobs they have been granted permissions on. Job-level permissions such as Build, Cancel and Read apply on top of it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0171","card":"q-jenkins-041-project-based-permissions","sec":"jenkins-s042","q":"In Jenkins, why would you switch from the global Matrix Authorization Strategy to the Project-based Matrix Authorization Strategy?","a":"<p>A global matrix gives the same rights on every job. The project-based strategy adds a per-job or per-folder matrix, so different teams get different rights on different jobs (for example, team A can build and configure only its own jobs). Users then see only the jobs they have permission for.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0172","card":"q-jenkins-041-project-based-permissions","sec":"jenkins-s042","q":"In Jenkins, after you enable Project-based Matrix Authorization, how do the global permissions and a job's own permissions combine, and what does a restricted user still need globally?","a":"<p>With the default inheritance, a user's rights on a job are the global rights plus whatever the job's matrix grants, so the job-level matrix can only add rights. The user still needs the global <strong>Overall Read</strong> to log in and see anything, so the global matrix should give restricted users Overall Read and nothing more.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0173","card":"q-jenkins-041-project-based-permissions","sec":"jenkins-s042","q":"In Jenkins, what is the risk when you change the authorization strategy on a live controller, and how do you protect yourself?","a":"<p>A new strategy starts with an empty matrix, so settings from the old one do not carry over. If you do not grant yourself Administer first, you lock yourself out. Add your admin user with Overall Administer before saving, keep a backup of <code>config.xml</code>, and test with a second browser session. If locked out, you can fix it by editing <code>config.xml</code> on disk and restarting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0174","card":"q-jenkins-041-project-based-permissions","sec":"jenkins-s042","q":"In Jenkins, when would you use the Role-Based Authorization Strategy plugin instead of a project-based matrix?","a":"<p>Use roles when many users share the same set of rights. You define global roles, plus item roles that match job names by regex, and assign users or groups to roles. A project matrix means editing every job by hand, which does not scale across hundreds of jobs. Roles are reusable and easier to audit. The cost is an extra plugin to maintain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0175","card":"q-jenkins-042-challenge-isolate-engineering-and-financ","sec":"jenkins-s043","q":"You must make sure two teams on one Jenkins controller cannot see or change each other's jobs. How do you set it up?","a":"<p>Put each team's jobs in its own folder. Use Project-based Matrix (or Role-Based) authorization, give each team user or group Overall Read globally, and grant the full job permissions only on that team's folder. Remove anonymous access. Then log in as a user of each team and confirm that the other folder is invisible and Manage Jenkins is not available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0176","card":"q-jenkins-042-challenge-isolate-engineering-and-financ","sec":"jenkins-s043","q":"In Jenkins, why does folder-level permission isolation not give full isolation between teams, and what do you add?","a":"<p>Both teams still share one controller: the same plugins, the same JVM, global credentials and the same agents. A job from one team could consume all executors or reach global credentials. Add folder-scoped credentials, agent labels or separate agent pools per team, and quotas. For strong isolation (regulated data, untrusted teams) use separate controllers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0177","card":"q-jenkins-043-credentials-store-and-scopes","sec":"jenkins-s044","q":"What is the Credentials provider, and which types can it store?","a":"<p>The Credentials plugin stores secrets encrypted in Jenkins and exposes them to jobs by ID, never as plain text. Types: username and password, SSH private key, secret text (API keys, tokens), secret file and certificates. Credentials can be domain-restricted.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0178","card":"q-jenkins-043-credentials-store-and-scopes","sec":"jenkins-s044","q":"What is the difference between credential scopes?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Global</strong>: usable by any job or pipeline on the instance.</li>\n  <li><strong>System</strong>: usable only by Jenkins itself (connecting to agents, email), not exposed to job builds.</li>\n  <li><strong>Item/Folder</strong>: scoped to one folder or job (least privilege, good for team isolation).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0179","card":"q-jenkins-043-credentials-store-and-scopes","sec":"jenkins-s044","q":"In Jenkins, why is it better to reference a credential by its ID in a Jenkinsfile than to put the secret value in the job?","a":"<p>The Jenkinsfile only contains the ID, so the secret never reaches Git, build logs or job configs. To rotate the secret you update the one credential entry and every job that uses the ID picks up the new value without any code change. Access can also be controlled by scope and by who may use the credential.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0180","card":"q-jenkins-043-credentials-store-and-scopes","sec":"jenkins-s044","q":"Where would you store a deployment key that only one team's folder may use, and why?","a":"<p>Store it as a credential in that folder's own credentials store (folder scope), not in the global one. Only jobs inside the folder can use it, so other teams cannot read it. This follows least privilege and limits the damage if some other job is compromised.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0181","card":"q-jenkins-043-credentials-store-and-scopes","sec":"jenkins-s044","q":"In Jenkins, a pipeline cannot find a credential with the right ID that you just created in Manage Jenkins. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check the scope. A credential with System scope is not available to builds. A credential created inside a folder is visible only to jobs in that folder. Check the ID spelling, the credential type (a username and password credential cannot be bound as secret text), and whether the credential has a domain that restricts the URL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0182","card":"q-jenkins-044-using-credentials-in-a-jenkinsfile","sec":"jenkins-s045","q":"How do you inject credentials securely?","a":"<p><code>withCredentials</code> binds a stored credential to environment variables for the duration of a block, then unbinds it, and Jenkins masks the values in logs. Alternatively use <code>credentials('id')</code> in an <code>environment</code> block. For username and password credentials it sets one variable holding <code>username:password</code> plus two derived variables, <code>&lt;NAME&gt;_USR</code> and <code>&lt;NAME&gt;_PSW</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: &#x27;dockerhub&#x27;,\n    usernameVariable: &#x27;USER&#x27;, passwordVariable: &#x27;PASS&#x27;)]) {\n  sh &#x27;echo &quot;$PASS&quot; | docker login -u &quot;$USER&quot; --password-stdin&#x27;\n}\n// or environment block:\nenvironment { AWS_KEY = credentials(&#x27;aws-access-key&#x27;) }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    environment {\n        MY_CRED = credentials(&#x27;my-credential-id&#x27;)   // username:password\n    }\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Example&#x27;) {\n            steps {\n                echo &quot;Username: ${env.MY_CRED_USR}&quot;  // MY_CRED_PSW holds the password\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Secret text with withCredentials\nwithCredentials([string(credentialsId: &#x27;my-api-key&#x27;, variable: &#x27;API_KEY&#x27;)]) {\n    sh &#x27;echo $API_KEY&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use the string form of <code>withCredentials</code> for secret text credentials. Recreate the permission matrix when switching strategies and test as a restricted user.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0183","card":"q-jenkins-044-using-credentials-in-a-jenkinsfile","sec":"jenkins-s045","q":"In a Jenkins Declarative Pipeline, what variables does <code>environment { REG = credentials('registry-login') }</code> create when the credential is a username with password?","a":"<p>Jenkins sets <code>REG</code> to <code>user:password</code> and also creates <code>REG_USR</code> and <code>REG_PSW</code> for the two parts. Use <code>$REG_USR</code> and <code>$REG_PSW</code> in <code>sh</code> steps. The values are masked in the console log, but keep the shell string single-quoted so Groovy does not interpolate the secret.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0184","card":"q-jenkins-044-using-credentials-in-a-jenkinsfile","sec":"jenkins-s045","q":"In this Jenkins step, why is the shell command in single quotes and not in double quotes?","a":"<p>In a single-quoted string Groovy does not interpolate, so the shell receives the literal <code>$PASS</code> and reads the environment variable itself. With double quotes Groovy would put the secret into the command string first. Jenkins warns about this, because the value can then leak through the Groovy process and logs. The shell-side expansion keeps the secret out of the Groovy layer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: &#x27;dockerhub&#x27;,\n    usernameVariable: &#x27;USER&#x27;, passwordVariable: &#x27;PASS&#x27;)]) {\n  sh &#x27;echo &quot;$PASS&quot; | docker login -u &quot;$USER&quot; --password-stdin&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0185","card":"q-jenkins-044-using-credentials-in-a-jenkinsfile","sec":"jenkins-s045","q":"When a Jenkins pipeline runs <code>docker login</code> in an <code>sh</code> step with a bound password, why use <code>--password-stdin</code> instead of <code>-p \"$PASS\"</code>?","a":"<p>A password passed with <code>-p</code> appears in the process list and in shell history, so other users on the agent could read it. <code>--password-stdin</code> reads it from standard input, which avoids that. Docker also warns that <code>-p</code> is insecure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0186","card":"q-jenkins-044-using-credentials-in-a-jenkinsfile","sec":"jenkins-s045","q":"In a Declarative Pipeline, what is the difference between binding a credential in <code>environment { X = credentials('id') }</code> at the top and in a <code>withCredentials</code> block?","a":"<p>A credential in the top-level <code>environment</code> is available to every stage for the whole run. <code>withCredentials</code> binds it only inside its block and removes it afterwards, so the secret lives for a shorter time and in a smaller scope. Use <code>withCredentials</code> for a single deploy step. Use <code>environment</code> only when many stages truly need the value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0187","card":"q-jenkins-044-using-credentials-in-a-jenkinsfile","sec":"jenkins-s045","q":"Which <code>withCredentials</code> binding do you use for an API token, for a kubeconfig file and for a username with password?","a":"<p>An API token is a secret text credential, bound with <code>string(credentialsId: ..., variable: ...)</code>. A kubeconfig is a secret file credential, bound with <code>file(credentialsId: ..., variable: ...)</code>, and the variable holds the temporary file path. A username with password uses <code>usernamePassword(credentialsId: ..., usernameVariable: ..., passwordVariable: ...)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0188","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"Why never hardcode tokens or passwords in steps, and how do you mask output?","a":"<p>Hardcoded secrets leak into SCM, build logs and job config and cannot be rotated centrally. Use the Credentials store with <code>withCredentials</code> (Jenkins masks bound values in logs). The Mask Passwords plugin can redact custom secret strings. Do not <code>echo</code> secrets.</p>\n<p>Several credentials can be bound in one <code>withCredentials</code> block:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withCredentials([\n  usernamePassword(credentialsId: &#x27;docker&#x27;, usernameVariable: &#x27;U&#x27;, passwordVariable: &#x27;P&#x27;),\n  string(credentialsId: &#x27;npm-token&#x27;, variable: &#x27;NPM_TOKEN&#x27;)\n]) {\n  sh &#x27;echo &quot;$P&quot; | docker login -u &quot;$U&quot; --password-stdin&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0189","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"What does the SSH Agent plugin do?","a":"<p>It starts an <code>ssh-agent</code> for a build and loads a stored SSH private-key credential into it, so <code>git</code>, <code>ssh</code> and <code>scp</code> authenticate without the key being written to disk. The key is available only inside the wrapped block.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sshagent([&#x27;deploy-ssh-key&#x27;]) {\n  sh &#x27;ssh deploy@server &quot;systemctl restart app&quot;&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0190","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"How do you integrate Jenkins with Vault or AWS Secrets Manager?","a":"<p>Use the HashiCorp Vault or AWS Secrets Manager Credentials Provider plugin. Secrets stay in the external manager (single source of truth, rotation, audit) and are fetched at runtime, so there are no long-lived copies in Jenkins.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withVault(vaultSecrets: [[path: &#x27;secret/app&#x27;, secretValues: [[envVar: &#x27;DB_PASS&#x27;, vaultKey: &#x27;password&#x27;]]]]) {\n  sh &#x27;deploy --password &quot;$DB_PASS&quot;&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0191","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"Jenkins masks bound credentials in the console log. Is that enough protection, and where does it fail?","a":"<p>No. Masking replaces only the exact secret string in the output. If the pipeline prints a transformed value (base64, URL-encoded, split in pieces), it is not masked. Anyone who can configure a job or a Jenkinsfile can also write code that sends the secret elsewhere. Masking is a safety net. The real controls are least-privilege credentials, scoped access and review of pipeline changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0192","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"In Jenkins, why is <code>sshagent</code> preferred over copying a private key onto the agent's disk for a deploy step?","a":"<p><code>sshagent</code> loads the key into a temporary ssh-agent process for the duration of the block, so the key is never stored as a file in the workspace or the agent's home directory. Nothing is left behind for later builds to read, and the key is managed centrally as a Jenkins credential.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0193","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"What does this Jenkins step do, and why is it better than storing the database password as a Jenkins credential?","a":"<p>It reads the key <code>password</code> at the path <code>secret/app</code> from HashiCorp Vault when the block starts and exposes it to the commands inside as <code>DB_PASS</code>. The secret stays in Vault, so there is one source of truth with audit logs, rotation and short-lived access. Jenkins keeps no long-lived copy that can drift or leak.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withVault(vaultSecrets: [[path: &#x27;secret/app&#x27;, secretValues: [[envVar: &#x27;DB_PASS&#x27;, vaultKey: &#x27;password&#x27;]]]]) {\n  sh &#x27;deploy --password &quot;$DB_PASS&quot;&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0194","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"How should Jenkins authenticate to Vault, and why not use a static root token?","a":"<p>Use an identity that can be limited and expires: AppRole, a Kubernetes service account, or a cloud identity. Give it a Vault policy that allows reading only the paths the jobs need. A static root token never expires and gives full access, so if it leaks, everything leaks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0195","card":"q-jenkins-046-script-security-csrf-and-hardening","sec":"jenkins-s047","q":"What is Script Security, and why do some Groovy scripts need admin approval?","a":"<p>The Script Security plugin gates Groovy that runs on the controller. Sandboxed pipeline code is limited to safe APIs; anything outside the sandbox (or <code>@NonCPS</code> code using sensitive methods) goes to In-process Script Approval, where an admin must approve it. This stops arbitrary code from compromising the controller.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0196","card":"q-jenkins-046-script-security-csrf-and-hardening","sec":"jenkins-s047","q":"How do you restrict script capabilities with the Groovy Sandbox?","a":"<p>Run pipeline Groovy in the sandbox (the default for a Jenkinsfile from SCM). Only allowlisted, safe methods are allowed; anything else is blocked or needs admin approval, so pipeline code cannot call dangerous controller APIs or the filesystem directly.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0197","card":"q-jenkins-046-script-security-csrf-and-hardening","sec":"jenkins-s047","q":"A Jenkins pipeline fails with \"Scripts not permitted to use method ...\". What does it mean and what are your options?","a":"<p>The pipeline runs in the Groovy sandbox and called a method that is not on the allowlist. An administrator can approve that exact signature in Manage Jenkins, In-process Script Approval. Better options are to rewrite the code with standard pipeline steps, or to move the logic into a trusted Global Shared Library. Approve only after reading what the method does, since each approval widens what pipeline code can do on the controller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0198","card":"q-jenkins-046-script-security-csrf-and-hardening","sec":"jenkins-s047","q":"In Jenkins, why does the sandbox exist, and who is it protecting against?","a":"<p>Pipeline Groovy executes inside the controller JVM. Without a sandbox, anyone who can edit a Jenkinsfile could call any Java API, read credentials and files, or run commands on the controller. The sandbox restricts scripts to an allowlist of safe calls, so developers can write pipelines without gaining control of the server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0199","card":"q-jenkins-046-script-security-csrf-and-hardening","sec":"jenkins-s047","q":"Why is unchecking \"Use Groovy Sandbox\" for a pipeline job a bad idea?","a":"<p>The script then runs with full controller privileges, and only admins may save it. A Jenkinsfile from SCM is not even allowed to opt out. Turning it off for convenience removes the main defense against code execution on the controller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0200","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"How do you protect the controller against CSRF?","a":"<p>Enable CSRF protection with the crumb issuer (on by default in modern Jenkins). State-changing requests must carry a valid crumb, which blocks forged cross-site requests. API clients fetch a crumb first, or use API tokens, which are crumb-exempt for scripted calls.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0201","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"What is Agent-to-Controller security?","a":"<p>Agents (possibly less trusted) can ask the controller to run commands or read files. Agent-to-Controller security restricts this with a configurable allow and deny list of commands and file access, which limits the blast radius if an agent is compromised.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0202","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"How do you disable anonymous read access?","a":"<p>In the authorization strategy (matrix or role), remove all permissions from the Anonymous user (do not grant Overall Read). Then only authenticated users can view Jenkins. Create a real admin account first so you do not lock yourself out.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0203","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"How do you audit user actions and job changes?","a":"<p>Install the <strong>Audit Trail</strong> plugin to log who did what (config changes, triggers, deletions) to a file, syslog or console. Combine it with <strong>Job Configuration History</strong> to track and diff config changes over time.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0204","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"What security considerations apply to untrusted code from GitHub PRs?","a":"<p>PRs from forks can carry a malicious Jenkinsfile or build scripts. Require approval to build fork PRs (use the trusted strategy under \"Discover PRs from forks\"), never expose production credentials to PR builds, run PR builds on isolated ephemeral agents, use least-privilege scoped credentials, and avoid sandbox bypass. Treat PR code as hostile.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0205","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"A script posts to the Jenkins API with <code>curl -X POST</code> and gets 403 \"No valid crumb was included in the request\". What is happening and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>CSRF protection is on, and the request has no crumb. Authenticate with a user and API token, which since Jenkins 2.176.2 does not need a crumb (older versions do). Alternatively, fetch a crumb from the <code>crumbIssuer</code> endpoint and send it as a request header with the same session. Do not turn CSRF protection off to fix the error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0206","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"Why is it dangerous if the controller trusts everything an agent asks of it?","a":"<p>Agents often run code from builds, including code from less trusted branches or PRs. A compromised agent could ask the controller to read files such as <code>secrets/</code> or run commands. The Agent to Controller access control limits what an agent may request, so one compromised build machine does not become a compromised controller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0207","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"In Jenkins, what is the difference between the Audit Trail plugin and the Job Configuration History plugin?","a":"<p>Audit Trail logs who did what (logins, builds triggered, config saves, deletions) to a log file, syslog or the console, which answers \"who did it and when\". Job Configuration History stores each version of a job or system config so you can diff and restore it, which answers \"what changed\". Teams usually run both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0208","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"Why must production credentials never be bound in a build triggered by a pull request from a fork?","a":"<p>The author of a fork PR controls the code, including the Jenkinsfile and the build scripts. They can print or send out any secret the build can read. Build fork PRs only after approval, on isolated ephemeral agents, with no production credentials, and let only trusted branches reach the deploy stages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0209","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"In Jenkins, what is the difference between a node, an agent and an executor?","a":"<p>A node is a machine (or the controller itself) that Jenkins can run builds on. The agent is the process on that machine that connects to the controller and runs the commands. An executor is one slot on a node that runs one build at a time, so a node with 4 executors runs 4 concurrent builds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0210","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"Why should a production Jenkins controller run builds on agents and not on its own executors?","a":"<p>Builds use CPU, memory and disk, and they run code that may be untrusted. On the controller they compete with scheduling and the web UI, and a bad build can read controller secrets. Set the controller's executors to 0 and run builds on agents, so a build problem cannot take Jenkins down or expose its data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0211","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"When do you use static agents, and when dynamic agents such as Docker or Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Static agents (SSH or inbound) suit special hardware, licensed tools, or Windows and macOS builds that need a long-lived machine. Dynamic agents suit bursty load and clean builds: each build gets a fresh container or pod that disappears afterwards, and you pay only while it runs. The trade-offs are startup time and loss of local caches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0212","card":"q-jenkins-049-pipeline-considerations-for-agents","sec":"jenkins-s050","q":"How do you manage tool installations across different agents?","a":"<p>Define installers in Global Tool Configuration and reference them with a <code>tools</code> block.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n  agent any\n  tools { maven &#x27;Maven-3.9&#x27;; jdk &#x27;JDK-17&#x27; }   // names from Global Tool Config\n  stages { stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;mvn -v &amp;&amp; mvn package&#x27; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0213","card":"q-jenkins-049-pipeline-considerations-for-agents","sec":"jenkins-s050","q":"In a Scripted Jenkins Pipeline running <code>node('linux') { sh 'make' }</code> from SCM, why does <code>make</code> fail to find the source files, and what changes in Declarative Pipeline?","a":"<p>Jenkins reads the Jenkinsfile on its own, but a Scripted <code>node</code> block gives you a fresh, empty workspace on the agent. Nothing fetches the code until you call <code>checkout scm</code> (or <code>git</code>). Declarative Pipeline does an implicit <code>checkout scm</code> at the start of each stage's agent, so the files are already there. Turn that off with <code>options { skipDefaultCheckout() }</code>, then you must check out explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0214","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"What does the agent machine need so that Jenkins can launch it over SSH?","a":"<p>A reachable SSH server, a user that Jenkins can log in as with a key, a recent Java runtime (the agent is a Java program), and a remote root directory where that user may write. Jenkins copies <code>agent.jar</code> over SSH and starts it, so no inbound port to the controller is required.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0215","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"In Jenkins, why is the Host Key Verification Strategy \"Non verifying\" a security weakness for an SSH agent?","a":"<p>Jenkins accepts any host key without checking it, so it cannot detect a man-in-the-middle or a different machine behind the same address. Use \"Known hosts file\" or \"Manually trusted key\" verification in production so that Jenkins connects only to servers whose key it has verified.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0216","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"In Jenkins node configuration, what does the usage setting \"Only build jobs with label expressions matching this node\" do compared to \"Use this node as much as possible\"?","a":"<p>With \"Only build jobs with label expressions matching this node\" the node runs only jobs that request its label, so it is reserved for specific work. With \"Use this node as much as possible\" it also takes any job without a label. Use the strict mode for special-purpose agents, so general jobs do not take their capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0217","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"Why should the user that Jenkins uses to log in to an SSH agent be a dedicated, non-root user?","a":"<p>Builds run with that user's rights. A dedicated unprivileged user limits what a malicious or buggy build can read or change on the machine, and keeps the Jenkins files separate from system files. Give it only the permissions the builds need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0218","card":"q-jenkins-051-docker-agents","sec":"jenkins-s052","q":"In a Jenkins Declarative Pipeline with a per-stage <code>docker</code> agent, what does <code>reuseNode true</code> do, and why is it needed?","a":"<p>With <code>reuseNode true</code> the container runs on the same node and in the same workspace directory as the top-level agent. Without it, the stage gets a new workspace on a node chosen for that stage, so files checked out earlier are missing. Use it when a stage needs only a different toolchain on the already checked-out code.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n  agent { label &#x27;build-node&#x27; }\n  stages {\n    stage(&#x27;Test&#x27;) {\n      agent {\n        docker { image &#x27;maven:3.8.4&#x27;; reuseNode true }\n      }\n      steps { sh &#x27;mvn -B test&#x27; }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0219","card":"q-jenkins-051-docker-agents","sec":"jenkins-s052","q":"What is the benefit of <code>agent { docker { image 'maven:3.8.4' } }</code> over a static agent that has Maven installed?","a":"<p>Each build starts in a fresh container with exactly the tools in the image, so builds are isolated and reproducible, with no state left from earlier runs. The tool version is in the Jenkinsfile and not on the machine. The node needs only Docker. The costs are image pull time and no local cache between runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0220","card":"q-jenkins-051-docker-agents","sec":"jenkins-s052","q":"In a Jenkins <code>docker</code> agent, why is <code>image 'maven:latest'</code> a bad choice?","a":"<p><code>latest</code> is a moving tag, so the same Jenkinsfile can build with a different Maven or JDK next month and break without any code change. Pin an explicit version tag (or a digest) so builds stay repeatable and upgrades are deliberate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0221","card":"q-jenkins-051-docker-agents","sec":"jenkins-s052","q":"In Jenkins, what do you need on a node before the <code>docker</code> agent keyword works?","a":"<p>The Docker Pipeline plugin installed on the controller, and a Docker engine on the node with the Jenkins agent user allowed to use it (for example, member of the <code>docker</code> group). Without the plugin the <code>docker</code> keyword is unknown, and without permission on the Docker socket the container cannot start.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0222","card":"q-jenkins-051-docker-agents","sec":"jenkins-s052","q":"Why is giving the Jenkins user access to the Docker socket a security concern?","a":"<p>Anyone who can run Docker commands can start a privileged container that mounts the host filesystem, which makes them effectively root on that node. Treat Docker access on an agent like root access: use dedicated agents, ephemeral machines, and do not run untrusted PR code there.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0223","card":"q-jenkins-051-docker-agents","sec":"jenkins-s052","q":"In Jenkins Declarative Pipeline, when would you use <code>agent { dockerfile true }</code> instead of <code>agent { docker { image '...' } }</code>?","a":"<p><code>dockerfile true</code> builds the agent image from a Dockerfile in the repository and runs the stages inside it. Use it when the project needs its own toolchain that no public image provides, and you want the build environment versioned with the code. The cost is an image build at the start of each run, unless the layers are cached.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0224","card":"q-jenkins-052-speeding-up-a-docker-agent-build-with-a","sec":"jenkins-s053","q":"A build in a Jenkins <code>docker</code> agent downloads all dependencies on every run. Why does this happen, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Dependency caches live inside the container's filesystem, which is destroyed when the build ends. Persist the cache outside the container: point the tool's cache directory at the workspace (which Jenkins mounts into the container), or mount a Docker volume or host directory into the container with <code>args</code>. The next build then reuses it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0225","card":"q-jenkins-052-speeding-up-a-docker-agent-build-with-a","sec":"jenkins-s053","q":"In Jenkins, how would you keep the Maven repository between builds in a docker agent? Complete the missing line.","a":"<p>Add an <code>args</code> line that mounts a volume at Maven's repository location, for example <code>args '-v maven-cache:/root/.m2'</code>. The named volume survives the container, so dependencies are downloaded only on the first build.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent {\n    docker {\n        image &#x27;maven:3.8.4&#x27;\n        ...\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0226","card":"q-jenkins-052-speeding-up-a-docker-agent-build-with-a","sec":"jenkins-s053","q":"What are the risks of persisting build caches in the workspace or in a shared volume?","a":"<p>A <code>cleanWs</code> step removes the cache from the workspace. Concurrent builds on the same volume can corrupt or race on it. A poisoned or stale cache can hide dependency problems, so keep a way to clear it and run a clean build regularly. Do not put untrusted PR builds on the same cache as release builds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0227","card":"q-jenkins-053-kubernetes-and-cloud-agents","sec":"jenkins-s054","q":"What is the Kubernetes plugin, and how does it spin up agents?","a":"<p>It runs each build in an ephemeral pod created on demand in a cluster and deletes it afterwards. You define a cloud pointing at the cluster and pod templates, and Jenkins scales agents elastically with zero idle cost, ideal for bursty CI.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent {\n  kubernetes {\n    yaml &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n      apiVersion: v1\n      kind: Pod\n      spec:\n        containers:\n        - name: maven\n          image: maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17\n          command: [&#x27;sleep&#x27;]\n          args: [&#x27;9999999&#x27;]\n    &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0228","card":"q-jenkins-053-kubernetes-and-cloud-agents","sec":"jenkins-s054","q":"In a Jenkins Kubernetes pod template, why do build containers often have <code>command: ['sleep']</code> or <code>cat</code> with <code>tty: true</code>?","a":"<p>A build container such as <code>maven</code> has no long-running process of its own, so it would exit at once and the pod would end. A command like <code>sleep</code> or <code>cat</code> keeps it alive, and Jenkins then runs the <code>sh</code> steps inside it with <code>container('name')</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0229","card":"q-jenkins-053-kubernetes-and-cloud-agents","sec":"jenkins-s054","q":"In the Jenkins Kubernetes plugin, what is the <code>jnlp</code> container in a pod, and who adds it?","a":"<p>It is the agent container that connects back to the Jenkins controller and executes the pipeline steps. The plugin adds it automatically to the pod. Other containers in the pod serve as tool environments, which steps select with <code>container('name')</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0230","card":"q-jenkins-053-kubernetes-and-cloud-agents","sec":"jenkins-s054","q":"What are the trade-offs of Kubernetes agents compared to permanent VM agents?","a":"<p>Benefits: elastic scale, no idle cost, a clean environment for every build, and per-build tool versions. Costs: pod start-up delay, no local caches unless you add volumes, extra cluster configuration (RBAC, network access to the controller), and building Docker images needs a rootless tool because there is no Docker daemon.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0231","card":"q-jenkins-053-kubernetes-and-cloud-agents","sec":"jenkins-s054","q":"In Jenkins, how do you define the pod for a Kubernetes agent: in the UI or in the Jenkinsfile, and which is better?","a":"<p>Both work. Pod templates can be defined on the Kubernetes cloud in the UI, or inline in the Jenkinsfile with <code>agent { kubernetes { yaml ... } }</code>. The inline form keeps the build environment versioned with the code and lets each project choose its own tools, so it is usually better. Shared templates in the UI suit a fixed standard environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0232","card":"q-jenkins-054-ephemeral-agents","sec":"jenkins-s055","q":"Why are dynamic ephemeral agents ideal for the cloud?","a":"<p>They are created per build and destroyed after, so there are no long-lived nodes to patch and no leaked state. Benefits: a clean reproducible environment per run, elastic scale (pay only while building) and no snowflake agents. They suit Kubernetes and EC2, where compute is on demand.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0233","card":"q-jenkins-054-ephemeral-agents","sec":"jenkins-s055","q":"What problems do long-lived Jenkins agents have that ephemeral agents avoid?","a":"<p>Long-lived agents collect leftover files, changed tool versions and manual fixes (\"snowflake\" machines), so builds start to depend on the machine's history. They also need patching and sit idle. Ephemeral agents are created for one build and destroyed, so every build starts from the same image and no secrets or files can leak to the next build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0234","card":"q-jenkins-054-ephemeral-agents","sec":"jenkins-s055","q":"What do you lose with ephemeral Jenkins agents, and how do you reduce that cost?","a":"<p>You lose warm caches (dependencies, layers) and pay a start-up delay for each build. Reduce both with prebuilt agent images that include the tools, a shared cache volume or a proxy repository such as Nexus or Artifactory, and a small pool of warm agents if start-up time matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0235","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"How do you run several containers (Maven, AWS CLI) in one pod?","a":"<p>Declare multiple containers in the pod YAML and target each with <code>container('name')</code>; they share the workspace volume.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent {\n  kubernetes { yaml &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - {name: maven, image: maven:3.9, command: [cat], tty: true}\n      - {name: aws,   image: amazon/aws-cli, command: [cat], tty: true}\n  &#x27;&#x27;&#x27; }\n}\nstages {\n  stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;)  { steps { container(&#x27;maven&#x27;) { sh &#x27;mvn package&#x27; } } }\n  stage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;) { steps { container(&#x27;aws&#x27;)   { sh &#x27;aws s3 cp target/app.jar s3://bucket/&#x27; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0236","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"How do you provision EC2 instances as agents with the AWS EC2 plugin?","a":"<p>Configure an EC2 cloud with an AMI, instance type, IAM role, subnet and security group, and labels. When jobs need capacity Jenkins launches EC2 instances as agents (over SSH) and terminates idle ones per the retention strategy.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0237","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"How does workspace persistence work in Kubernetes pods?","a":"<p>By default an ephemeral pod's workspace vanishes with the pod. Mount a PVC (or use <code>workspaceVolume: dynamicPVC(...)</code>) so the workspace persists across steps, containers or reruns, which helps caches such as <code>.m2</code> and <code>node_modules</code>. Trade-offs: PVC lifecycle and cleanup, and RWX access constraints.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0238","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"Why run Maven and the AWS CLI as two containers in one pod instead of one large image that contains both?","a":"<p>Each container has one tool and one version, which is easy to update and reuse in other pipelines. Small images start faster, and a vulnerability in one tool does not spread to the others. Since the containers share the pod's workspace volume, each stage can use the right tool with <code>container('name')</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0239","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"In this Jenkins pod definition, how do the maven and aws containers see the same files?","a":"<p>The Kubernetes plugin mounts a shared workspace volume (an <code>emptyDir</code> by default) into every container of the pod, including the <code>jnlp</code> agent container. A file that <code>mvn package</code> writes is therefore visible to the other containers at the same path. The data disappears when the pod ends.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- {name: maven, image: maven:3.9, command: [cat], tty: true}\n- {name: aws, image: amazon/aws-cli, command: [cat], tty: true}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0240","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"When would you use the EC2 plugin for Jenkins agents rather than Kubernetes agents?","a":"<p>Use EC2 when you have no Kubernetes cluster, or when builds need a full VM: nested virtualization, special AMIs, GPUs, Windows or macOS images. EC2 agents start more slowly than pods, but they give full machine isolation. Spot instances can cut cost, with the risk that AWS interrupts a build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0241","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"How should the Jenkins controller get permission to start EC2 agents?","a":"<p>Attach an IAM role (instance profile) to the controller with only the EC2 permissions the plugin needs, such as running and terminating instances in the intended subnets. Do not store long-lived access keys in Jenkins when a role is available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0242","card":"q-jenkins-056-building-images-in-kubernetes-agents","sec":"jenkins-s057","q":"How do you run Docker-in-Docker or Kaniko in Kubernetes agents to build images?","a":"<p>DinD runs a Docker daemon in a sidecar and needs privileged mode, which is a security concern. Kaniko builds images in userspace without a Docker daemon or privileged access, so it is preferred in Kubernetes. It pushes straight to a registry using mounted credentials.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>container(&#x27;kaniko&#x27;) {\n  sh &#x27;/kaniko/executor --context `pwd` --dockerfile Dockerfile --destination registry/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>A pod that builds with Maven and then pushes an image with Kaniko (the <code>debug</code> executor image provides a shell):</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n  agent { kubernetes { yaml &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - {name: maven,  image: maven:3.9,       command: [cat], tty: true}\n      - {name: kaniko, image: gcr.io/kaniko-project/executor:debug, command: [cat], tty: true}\n  &#x27;&#x27;&#x27; } }\n  stages {\n    stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { container(&#x27;maven&#x27;)  { sh &#x27;mvn package&#x27; } } }\n    stage(&#x27;Image&#x27;) { steps { container(&#x27;kaniko&#x27;) {\n      sh &#x27;/kaniko/executor --context `pwd` --dockerfile Dockerfile --destination reg/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;\n    } } }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0243","card":"q-jenkins-056-building-images-in-kubernetes-agents","sec":"jenkins-s057","q":"Why can a build inside a Jenkins Kubernetes pod not simply run <code>docker build</code>?","a":"<p>There is no Docker daemon in the pod, and modern clusters use containerd or CRI-O as the runtime, so there is no daemon socket on the node to share either. Mounting the host's Docker socket is not available there and would give the pod control over the node. Use a daemonless builder such as Kaniko or Buildah, or a remote build service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0244","card":"q-jenkins-056-building-images-in-kubernetes-agents","sec":"jenkins-s057","q":"In this Kaniko step, what do <code>--context</code> and <code>--destination</code> do?","a":"<p><code>--context</code> is the directory that holds the build files (here the current workspace). <code>--dockerfile</code> names the Dockerfile in it. <code>--destination</code> is the image name and tag to push to when the build finishes. Using <code>$BUILD_NUMBER</code> gives every build a unique, traceable tag.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sh &#x27;/kaniko/executor --context `pwd` --dockerfile Dockerfile --destination registry/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0245","card":"q-jenkins-056-building-images-in-kubernetes-agents","sec":"jenkins-s057","q":"How does Kaniko authenticate to the registry when it pushes an image from a Jenkins pod?","a":"<p>Through a Docker <code>config.json</code> with registry credentials, mounted into the Kaniko container (usually at <code>/kaniko/.docker/</code>) from a Kubernetes secret. On a cloud registry you can use a workload identity or credential helper instead of a static password.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0246","card":"q-jenkins-056-building-images-in-kubernetes-agents","sec":"jenkins-s057","q":"In Jenkins, why does the Kaniko container use the <code>debug</code> executor image and the same <code>command: [cat]</code> pattern?","a":"<p>The plain Kaniko executor image has no shell, so Jenkins cannot run <code>sh</code> steps in it. The <code>debug</code> variant includes a busybox shell. <code>cat</code> with <code>tty: true</code> keeps the container running so the pipeline can run the executor command in it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0247","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"What is the difference between launch methods?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Launch agent via SSH</strong>: the controller SSHes out to start the agent. Simple for Linux hosts the controller can reach.</li>\n  <li><strong>Launch agent by connecting it to the controller (Inbound/JNLP)</strong>: the agent runs <code>agent.jar</code> and initiates the connection. Good behind NAT or firewalls, and for Windows.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cloud plugins</strong>: Kubernetes, EC2 or Docker create agents on demand.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The choice depends on network reachability, OS and whether agents are static or dynamic.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0248","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"How do you set retention strategies for dynamic agents?","a":"<p>They control the agent lifecycle: <strong>Always</strong> (keep up), <strong>Demand</strong> (start when needed), <strong>Idle timeout</strong> (terminate after N idle minutes), or plugin-specific strategies. Short idle timeouts minimize cloud cost, and a small warm pool reduces cold-start latency.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0249","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"What does Node Offline mean, and what causes disconnects?","a":"<p>The controller lost contact with a node and cannot schedule builds there. Common causes: network or firewall issues, agent process crash or OOM, disk full on the agent, Java version mismatch, an expired credential or secret, or the node being taken offline for maintenance.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0250","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"In Jenkins, what is the WebSocket option for inbound agents, and why use it?","a":"<p>In Jenkins 2.217 and later, an inbound agent can connect to the controller over a WebSocket on the normal HTTP(S) port. It does not need the separate inbound TCP port, so it works through reverse proxies and strict firewalls that only allow HTTP(S). Start the agent with the <code>-webSocket</code> option.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0251","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"A permanent Jenkins agent shows as offline after the machine was rebooted. What do you check?","a":"<p>Open the node's log for the error. Check that the agent process or service starts at boot (an inbound agent needs a service), that the SSH server is up for SSH agents, that credentials and host keys are still valid, that Java is installed, and that the disk is not full. For an inbound agent also check the secret and the network path to the controller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0252","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"When would you choose an idle-timeout retention strategy for cloud agents, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>When builds are bursty and compute costs money. The agent terminates after a set idle time, so you pay little when no jobs run. The trade-off is a cold start for the next build after the timeout, so keep a short idle time for cost and a longer one if start-up is slow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0253","card":"q-jenkins-058-what-a-shared-library-is","sec":"jenkins-s059","q":"What is a Shared Library, and why use it in enterprises?","a":"<p>A versioned Git repo of reusable pipeline code (steps, helpers, classes) that many Jenkinsfiles import. It enforces DRY, standardizes CI/CD across teams (one place to fix or upgrade), hides complexity behind simple custom steps and keeps each Jenkinsfile tiny.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0254","card":"q-jenkins-058-what-a-shared-library-is","sec":"jenkins-s059","q":"What is the structure of a Shared Library?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>vars/</code>: global custom steps and variables; each <code>foo.groovy</code> becomes a callable step <code>foo(...)</code>.</li>\n  <li><code>src/</code>: Groovy classes in package folders for OOP-style helper logic.</li>\n  <li><code>resources/</code>: non-Groovy files (templates, JSON, scripts) loaded with <code>libraryResource</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>(root)\n├── vars/          # deployApp.groovy  → step: deployApp(...)\n├── src/           # org/acme/Utils.groovy\n└── resources/     # org/acme/config.json</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0255","card":"q-jenkins-058-what-a-shared-library-is","sec":"jenkins-s059","q":"In Jenkins, when is a Shared Library worth it, and when is it too much?","a":"<p>It is worth it when several repositories repeat the same pipeline logic (build, scan, deploy) and you want one place to fix and standardize it. It is too much for one or two pipelines, where it adds a second repository, version management and testing effort. The risk is that a central change can break every team, so version it and release deliberately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0256","card":"q-jenkins-058-what-a-shared-library-is","sec":"jenkins-s059","q":"What is the difference between copying pipeline code into every Jenkinsfile and using a Shared Library?","a":"<p>Copied code drifts: every fix must be repeated in each repository and the copies diverge. A library has a single versioned source, so one change reaches all consumers when they update their version. The cost is a dependency between teams and the need to keep the library backward compatible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0257","card":"q-jenkins-058-what-a-shared-library-is","sec":"jenkins-s059","q":"Where do you register a Global Shared Library in Jenkins, and what do you configure?","a":"<p>In Manage Jenkins, System, Global Pipeline Libraries (called Global Trusted Pipeline Libraries in older releases). You give it a name, a default version (a branch, tag or commit) and a source such as a Git repository URL with credentials. Pipelines then load it by that name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0258","card":"q-jenkins-058-what-a-shared-library-is","sec":"jenkins-s059","q":"What is the directory layout of a Jenkins Shared Library, and what goes in each folder?","a":"<p><code>vars/</code> holds global steps and variables: each <code>name.groovy</code> becomes a callable step <code>name(...)</code>, with an optional <code>name.txt</code> for help text. <code>src/</code> holds regular Groovy classes in packages, for structured and unit-testable logic. <code>resources/</code> holds non-Groovy files such as templates, JSON and scripts, read with <code>libraryResource</code>. Use <code>vars</code> for the pipeline-facing API and <code>src</code> for the implementation behind it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0259","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"How do you load a library implicitly vs explicitly?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Implicit</strong>: a Global Shared Library configured with \"Load implicitly\" is available in every pipeline.</li>\n  <li><strong>Explicit</strong>: <code>@Library('my-lib@main') _</code> at the top of the Jenkinsfile, pinning a branch, tag or commit.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@Library(&#x27;shared-pipeline@v2.1&#x27;) _\npipeline { agent any; stages { stage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;){ steps { deployApp env: &#x27;prod&#x27; } } } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0260","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"How do you write a custom step in <code>vars/</code> with an argument map?","a":"<p>Define a <code>call</code> method taking a <code>Map</code> of named arguments with defaults; the file name becomes the step name.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// vars/deployApp.groovy\ndef call(Map args = [:]) {\n  String env     = args.env ?: &#x27;dev&#x27;\n  String version = args.version ?: env.BUILD_NUMBER\n  echo &quot;Deploying ${version} to ${env}&quot;\n  sh &quot;./deploy.sh ${env} ${version}&quot;\n}\n// usage: deployApp env: &#x27;prod&#x27;, version: &#x27;1.4.0&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0261","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"What is the difference between <code>vars/*.groovy</code> globals and <code>src/</code> classes?","a":"<p><code>vars</code> files expose a step-like global (with <code>call()</code> making it invocable as <code>name(args)</code>), good for the pipeline-facing DSL. <code>src</code> classes are regular Groovy or OOP for structured, reusable and unit-testable logic. Use <code>vars</code> for the API surface and <code>src</code> for the implementation.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0262","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"What is the difference between Global and Folder-level Shared Libraries?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Global</strong>: configured in Manage Jenkins, available instance-wide, can be trusted (runs outside the sandbox) and loaded implicitly.</li>\n  <li><strong>Folder-level</strong>: configured on a folder, scoped to jobs in it, always sandboxed and untrusted. Use for team-specific libraries and to limit the blast radius.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0263","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"In Jenkins global Shared Library settings, what do \"Load implicitly\" and \"Allow default version to be overridden\" change for pipelines?","a":"<p>\"Load implicitly\" makes every pipeline load the library at its default version without an <code>@Library</code> annotation, so its <code>vars/</code> steps are always available. \"Allow default version to be overridden\" lets a pipeline request another version with <code>@Library('name@ref')</code>. If it is off, the configured default version is always used, which gives central control over which library code runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0264","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"In Jenkins, why does the line <code>@Library('shared-pipeline@v2.1') _</code> end with an underscore?","a":"<p><code>@Library</code> is a Groovy annotation, and an annotation must be attached to a declaration. The underscore is a dummy variable that gives it something to attach to when you do not import a class. If you import a class after the annotation, the underscore is not needed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@Library(&#x27;shared-pipeline@v2.1&#x27;) _</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0265","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"What is the pitfall in this shared library step?","a":"<p>The local variable <code>env</code> hides Jenkins' global <code>env</code> object. Then <code>env.BUILD_NUMBER</code> is looked up on a String and fails with a missing property error. Rename the local variable (for example <code>targetEnv</code>) and keep <code>env.BUILD_NUMBER</code> for the build number.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def call(Map args = [:]) {\n  String env     = args.env ?: &#x27;dev&#x27;\n  String version = args.version ?: env.BUILD_NUMBER\n  ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0266","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"In Jenkins, how can a pipeline choose the library version at run time instead of with the <code>@Library</code> annotation?","a":"<p>Use the <code>library</code> step inside the pipeline, for example <code>library 'my-lib@main'</code>, or build the version string from a parameter. The annotation is resolved before the pipeline runs, so it cannot depend on a runtime value, while the step can. Use the step when the version comes from a parameter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0267","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"In a Shared Library <code>vars/deployApp.groovy</code> file, why is the method named <code>call</code>?","a":"<p>Groovy calls a <code>call</code> method when you invoke an object like a function. Jenkins exposes each file in <code>vars/</code> as a global variable named after the file, so a <code>call</code> method lets pipelines write <code>deployApp(...)</code> or <code>deployApp env: 'prod'</code> as if it were a built-in step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0268","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"How do you read a template or script file stored in the library's <code>resources/</code> folder from a step?","a":"<p>Use the <code>libraryResource</code> step with the path, for example <code>libraryResource 'org/acme/config.json'</code>. It returns the file content as a string. Write it to the workspace with <code>writeFile</code> if a tool needs a real file. This lets the library ship scripts and templates with its code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0269","card":"q-jenkins-060-groovy-in-pipelines-cps-and-noncps","sec":"jenkins-s061","q":"Which Groovy concepts matter for robust pipelines (CPS, <code>@NonCPS</code>)?","a":"<p>Pipeline Groovy is CPS-transformed (continuation-passing style) so builds can pause, resume and survive controller restarts. That means program state must be serializable and some Groovy features behave oddly. Mark methods <code>@NonCPS</code> when they do complex non-pausing work (iterating collections with closures, using non-serializable objects), and keep pipeline steps out of them.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@NonCPS\ndef parse(String json) { new groovy.json.JsonSlurper().parseText(json) }  // runs outside CPS</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0270","card":"q-jenkins-060-groovy-in-pipelines-cps-and-noncps","sec":"jenkins-s061","q":"What causes <code>NotSerializableException</code>, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>CPS serializes pipeline state at every step, so holding a non-serializable object (a <code>JsonSlurper</code> result type, a <code>Matcher</code>, streams) across a step boundary throws it. Fix by confining such objects to a <code>@NonCPS</code> method, nulling them before a step, or copying into serializable types (<code>HashMap</code>, <code>String</code>) before continuing.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0271","card":"q-jenkins-060-groovy-in-pipelines-cps-and-noncps","sec":"jenkins-s061","q":"Why can you not call pipeline steps such as <code>sh</code> or <code>echo</code> inside a method marked <code>@NonCPS</code>?","a":"<p><code>@NonCPS</code> code runs as normal Groovy and cannot be paused or saved, while steps are asynchronous and need the CPS machinery to suspend and resume the build. Calling a step from <code>@NonCPS</code> code fails or behaves wrongly. Keep <code>@NonCPS</code> methods pure computation and call steps outside them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0272","card":"q-jenkins-060-groovy-in-pipelines-cps-and-noncps","sec":"jenkins-s061","q":"In Jenkins, what does the pipeline durability setting <code>PERFORMANCE_OPTIMIZED</code> trade off?","a":"<p>It writes pipeline state to disk less often, which makes pipelines faster and reduces disk load. If the controller crashes (not a clean restart), a running pipeline may not be resumable. The default <code>MAX_SURVIVABILITY</code> favors resuming, and <code>PERFORMANCE_OPTIMIZED</code> favors speed. Set it with the <code>durabilityHint</code> option.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0273","card":"q-jenkins-060-groovy-in-pipelines-cps-and-noncps","sec":"jenkins-s061","q":"In Jenkins, why is the <code>readJSON</code> step often a better choice than parsing JSON with <code>JsonSlurper</code> in a pipeline?","a":"<p><code>readJSON</code> (from Pipeline Utility Steps) returns serializable objects, so they can be kept in variables across steps. <code>JsonSlurper</code> returns objects that may fail serialization and needs <code>@NonCPS</code> and sandbox approvals. Prefer the step, and use <code>JsonSlurper</code> only inside a <code>@NonCPS</code> method if you must.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0274","card":"q-jenkins-061-testing-and-versioning-libraries","sec":"jenkins-s062","q":"How do you unit test shared libraries?","a":"<p><code>JenkinsPipelineUnit</code> mocks the pipeline runtime so library code runs in plain JUnit or Spock tests, asserting which steps were called and with which arguments, without a live Jenkins. Register mock steps, load the library, execute and verify the call stack.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Spock-style\ndef &quot;deployApp echoes target&quot;() {\n  when:  helper.callClosure(&#x27;deployApp&#x27;, [env: &#x27;prod&#x27;])\n  then:  helper.callStack.any { it.methodName == &#x27;echo&#x27; &amp;&amp; it.args[0].contains(&#x27;prod&#x27;) }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0275","card":"q-jenkins-061-testing-and-versioning-libraries","sec":"jenkins-s062","q":"How do you version and test library changes without breaking pipelines?","a":"<p>Keep the library in Git and pin versions in <code>@Library('lib@v1.3')</code> so consumers do not auto-adopt breaking changes. Develop on a branch, test with <code>@Library('lib@feature-x')</code> in a sandbox job (and JenkinsPipelineUnit), then release a tag and bump consumers deliberately. Semantic versioning and a changelog keep upgrades safe.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0276","card":"q-jenkins-061-testing-and-versioning-libraries","sec":"jenkins-s062","q":"In Jenkins, why is pinning a Shared Library to a tag better than using <code>@Library('lib@main')</code> in production pipelines?","a":"<p>A tag always points to the same code, so a pipeline does not change unless someone edits its Jenkinsfile. With <code>main</code>, every merge to the library changes all consuming pipelines at once, and a bug breaks all of them. Tags let teams upgrade on their own schedule and roll back by changing the version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0277","card":"q-jenkins-061-testing-and-versioning-libraries","sec":"jenkins-s062","q":"Which parts of a Shared Library can be unit tested without Jenkins, and which need a Jenkins-like harness?","a":"<p>Plain Groovy classes in <code>src/</code> that do not call pipeline steps can be tested with ordinary JUnit or Spock tests. Code in <code>vars/</code> calls steps such as <code>sh</code> and <code>echo</code>, so it needs a harness such as JenkinsPipelineUnit that mocks them. Keeping logic in <code>src/</code> and the step calls in thin <code>vars/</code> wrappers makes most of it easy to test.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0278","card":"q-jenkins-061-testing-and-versioning-libraries","sec":"jenkins-s062","q":"How do you let a team try an unreleased library change without affecting other pipelines?","a":"<p>Push the change to a branch and have a test pipeline load it with <code>@Library('lib@my-branch') _</code>. The default version stays the same for everyone else. Once it works, merge, tag a release and let consumers move to the new tag.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0279","card":"q-jenkins-062-archiving-artifacts","sec":"jenkins-s063","q":"How do you archive artifacts, and what is the difference between artifacts and stashes?","a":"<p><code>archiveArtifacts</code> saves files to the controller, viewable and downloadable from the build page for the build's retention. A <strong>stash</strong> is temporary in-build storage for passing files between stages or agents and is deleted when the build ends. Archive for deliverables, stash for hand-offs within the pipeline.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>archiveArtifacts artifacts: &#x27;target/*.jar&#x27;, fingerprint: true</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0280","card":"q-jenkins-062-archiving-artifacts","sec":"jenkins-s063","q":"What is the difference between <code>stash</code>/<code>unstash</code> and <code>archiveArtifacts</code>?","a":"<p><code>stash</code>/<code>unstash</code> move files between stages or agents within one build (ephemeral, not shown in the UI). <code>archiveArtifacts</code> persists files to the controller and exposes them in the UI and API.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;make&#x27;; stash name: &#x27;app&#x27;, includes: &#x27;dist/**&#x27; } }\nstage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;){ agent { label &#x27;deployer&#x27; }; steps { unstash &#x27;app&#x27;; sh &#x27;./deploy.sh&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0281","card":"q-jenkins-062-archiving-artifacts","sec":"jenkins-s063","q":"Where does Jenkins store archived artifacts, and why is that not a good place for large binaries?","a":"<p>On the controller's disk, under the job's build directory in <code>JENKINS_HOME</code>. Large or many artifacts fill the disk, slow backups and load the controller, and they disappear with the build when a discarder deletes it. Store release binaries and Docker images in an artifact repository or a registry (Nexus, Artifactory, S3, ECR) and keep only small reports in Jenkins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0282","card":"q-jenkins-062-archiving-artifacts","sec":"jenkins-s063","q":"In Jenkins, what does the pattern <code>**/*.jar</code> in <code>archiveArtifacts</code> mean?","a":"<p>It is an Ant-style pattern. <code>**</code> matches any number of directories, so the pattern matches every <code>.jar</code> file under the workspace, at any depth. A pattern with no match archives nothing and fails the step, unless <code>allowEmptyArchive</code> is set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0283","card":"q-jenkins-062-archiving-artifacts","sec":"jenkins-s063","q":"In a Jenkins Pipeline, a build stage runs on one agent and a deploy stage on another. Should you use <code>stash</code>/<code>unstash</code> or <code>archiveArtifacts</code> to move the build output between them, and how do the two differ?","a":"<p>Use <code>stash</code> and <code>unstash</code> for hand-offs inside one run. A stash is temporary, lives only for that build, is not shown in the UI, and works across agents. <code>archiveArtifacts</code> keeps files on the controller for the build's retention, shows them on the build page and in the API, and lets other jobs and people download them. Use stash for intermediate files and archive for deliverables. Stash is meant for small or medium files because it goes through the controller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0284","card":"q-jenkins-063-sharing-artifacts-between-jobs","sec":"jenkins-s064","q":"In the Copy Artifact plugin, how do you select the latest successful build of a job and how do you select one particular build?","a":"<p>Use the selector <code>lastSuccessful()</code> for the latest successful build. For a particular build use <code>specific('42')</code>, which takes a build number (a string, often from a parameter). <code>lastCompleted()</code> picks the newest finished build whatever its result, and <code>upstream()</code> picks the build that triggered this one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0285","card":"q-jenkins-063-sharing-artifacts-between-jobs","sec":"jenkins-s064","q":"A downstream job should use exactly the artifact from the build that triggered it. Why is <code>lastSuccessful()</code> unreliable, and what do you use?","a":"<p>If other builds of the upstream job finish in between, <code>lastSuccessful()</code> may return a different build than the one that triggered you. Pass the upstream build number as a parameter and use <code>specific(...)</code>, or use the <code>upstream()</code> selector, so the exact artifact is copied.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0286","card":"q-jenkins-063-sharing-artifacts-between-jobs","sec":"jenkins-s064","q":"When would you use an artifact repository instead of the Copy Artifact plugin to pass files between jobs?","a":"<p>When artifacts are large, are needed across many jobs, teams or Jenkins instances, or must outlive the build's retention. A repository gives versioned storage, access control and no dependency on a build still existing on the controller. Copy Artifact is fine for small outputs between a few related jobs on one controller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0287","card":"q-jenkins-063-sharing-artifacts-between-jobs","sec":"jenkins-s064","q":"Why does Copy Artifact need an explicit permission such as <code>copyArtifactPermission</code> on the producing job?","a":"<p>Without it any job could read another job's output, including artifacts that contain private data. The producer lists the jobs that may copy from it, which keeps access restricted. Use a specific list, and <code>*</code> only when the artifacts are not sensitive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0288","card":"q-jenkins-064-fingerprinting","sec":"jenkins-s065","q":"What problem does Jenkins fingerprinting solve?","a":"<p>It lets you trace an artifact across jobs. Jenkins records the MD5 checksum of a file when a job creates it and when other jobs use it, so you can open a fingerprint and see which build produced the file and which builds used it. That helps when you need to know where a faulty library or binary ended up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0289","card":"q-jenkins-064-fingerprinting","sec":"jenkins-s065","q":"Is the MD5 checksum in Jenkins fingerprinting a security feature?","a":"<p>No. It identifies a file by its content, not to prove that nobody tampered with it, and MD5 is not collision-resistant. For integrity and trust use signed artifacts and SHA-256 checksums or signatures from your artifact repository.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0290","card":"q-jenkins-064-fingerprinting","sec":"jenkins-s065","q":"Fingerprints in Jenkins are recorded only if jobs opt in. How do a producer and a consumer job each record one?","a":"<p>The producer sets <code>fingerprint: true</code> on <code>archiveArtifacts</code> (or uses the <code>fingerprint</code> step on files). The consumer sets <code>fingerprintArtifacts: true</code> on <code>copyArtifacts</code>, or fingerprints the file it uses. Both must record the same file content, since the match is on the checksum.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0291","card":"q-jenkins-065-junit-reports-and-code-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s066","q":"How do you publish JUnit results and coverage to the UI?","a":"<p>Use <code>junit</code> for test results (trends, and the build goes UNSTABLE on failures) and the coverage plugin (<code>recordCoverage</code>, or <code>publishHTML</code> for HTML reports).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post {\n  always {\n    junit &#x27;target/surefire-reports/*.xml&#x27;\n    recordCoverage tools: [[parser: &#x27;JACOCO&#x27;, pattern: &#x27;target/site/jacoco/jacoco.xml&#x27;]]\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0292","card":"q-jenkins-065-junit-reports-and-code-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s066","q":"Why can a Python or JavaScript project use the Jenkins JUnit plugin even though JUnit is a Java tool?","a":"<p>The plugin reads the JUnit XML report format, which many test tools can write (pytest with <code>--junitxml</code>, Jest reporters, Go test converters). The test tool only has to write that XML and the plugin shows trends and failures, whatever the language.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0293","card":"q-jenkins-065-junit-reports-and-code-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s066","q":"In Jenkins, what is the difference between JaCoCo and Cobertura?","a":"<p>Both measure which lines of code your tests execute. JaCoCo works on Java bytecode and is the usual choice for Java and Kotlin. Cobertura is an older format that other tools also write (for example Python's <code>coverage.py</code>), so it is common for non-Java projects. Jenkins can display both with its coverage plugin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0294","card":"q-jenkins-065-junit-reports-and-code-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s066","q":"In Jenkins, which plugin do you use today to publish coverage, and what happened to the older one?","a":"<p>In recent Jenkins versions, use the Coverage plugin and its <code>recordCoverage</code> step, which reads JaCoCo, Cobertura and other formats. The older Code Coverage API plugin and its <code>publishCoverage</code> step are deprecated, so new pipelines should use <code>recordCoverage</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0295","card":"q-jenkins-065-junit-reports-and-code-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s066","q":"Does 90% code coverage mean that the code is well tested?","a":"<p>No. Coverage only shows which lines ran during tests, not whether the tests assert anything useful. A test with no assertions can reach high coverage. Use coverage to find untested code, set a minimum threshold as a quality gate, and judge test quality with reviews, mutation testing and meaningful assertions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0296","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"What does each option do in this pytest command?","a":"<p><code>--junitxml=report.xml</code> writes the test results in JUnit XML for the Jenkins <code>junit</code> step. <code>--cov=.</code> turns on coverage measurement for code under the current directory (this needs the <code>pytest-cov</code> plugin). <code>--cov-report=xml:coverage.xml</code> writes the coverage in Cobertura-style XML to <code>coverage.xml</code>, which Jenkins can publish.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sh &#x27;pytest --junitxml=report.xml --cov=. --cov-report=xml:coverage.xml&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0297","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"Why run a Python pipeline's <code>pip install</code> inside a virtual environment or a Docker agent instead of installing directly on the agent?","a":"<p>Installing into the agent's global Python lets projects overwrite each other's package versions and leaves state behind, so builds become hard to reproduce. A virtual environment (or a fresh container) isolates each project's dependencies. It also avoids needing root to install packages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0298","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"Why does a pipeline place a lint stage before the test stage?","a":"<p>Linting is fast and catches syntax and style errors in seconds, so the pipeline fails early without paying for a slow test run. This fail-fast order gives quicker feedback and saves agent time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0299","card":"q-jenkins-067-what-a-failing-test-does-to-the-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s068","q":"In a Jenkins Declarative Pipeline, how do you mark a build UNSTABLE from the pipeline code itself, without failing it?","a":"<p>Call the <code>unstable('message')</code> step, which sets the stage and build result to UNSTABLE and lets the run continue. Setting <code>currentBuild.result = 'UNSTABLE'</code> does the same for the build result. Use it for soft conditions such as a coverage drop that should be visible but should not block, and gate the deploy stage on the result if needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0300","card":"q-jenkins-067-what-a-failing-test-does-to-the-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s068","q":"In Jenkins, <code>sh 'pytest'</code> returns a non-zero exit code. What is the build result, and what do the later stages do?","a":"<p>The <code>sh</code> step fails, so the stage fails and the build result is FAILURE. Later stages are skipped unless they have <code>when</code> conditions that run anyway, but <code>post</code> sections still run. The <code>junit</code> step does not downgrade a FAILURE to UNSTABLE; it only sets UNSTABLE when the build was otherwise successful.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0301","card":"q-jenkins-067-what-a-failing-test-does-to-the-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s068","q":"How do you get a build marked UNSTABLE (and not FAILURE) when tests fail in Jenkins?","a":"<p>Make the test command itself succeed even when tests fail, then let <code>junit</code> set the result. For Maven use <code>-Dmaven.test.failure.ignore=true</code>, for other tools append <code>|| true</code>. When <code>junit</code> finds failed tests it marks the build UNSTABLE. A better option for control is <code>catchError(buildResult: 'UNSTABLE', stageResult: 'FAILURE')</code> around the test step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0302","card":"q-jenkins-067-what-a-failing-test-does-to-the-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s068","q":"When do you prefer a FAILURE over UNSTABLE for failing tests?","a":"<p>When failing tests must stop the pipeline from deploying or promoting a bad build. FAILURE halts the later stages by default. UNSTABLE is useful when you want to keep going (for example to collect reports from other test suites), but then add an explicit check so the deploy stage does not run on an unstable build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0303","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"In Maven, why does <code>mvn package</code> run the tests again after a separate <code>mvn test</code> stage, and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>The <code>package</code> phase comes after <code>test</code> in the Maven lifecycle, so running <code>package</code> runs compile and test first. To avoid running tests twice, skip them in the package stage with <code>-DskipTests</code>, or run a single <code>mvn package</code> and read the reports afterwards.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0304","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"Where does Maven write the test reports that the Jenkins <code>junit</code> step should read?","a":"<p>The Surefire plugin writes JUnit XML files to <code>target/surefire-reports/</code>, one <code>TEST-*.xml</code> per test class. A pattern such as <code>target/surefire-reports/*.xml</code> or <code>**/TEST-*.xml</code> picks them up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0305","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"In Jenkins, why use <code>archiveArtifacts</code> with <code>onlyIfSuccessful: true</code> for a JAR?","a":"<p>It archives a build output only from a build that completed successfully, so a broken or half-built JAR is never kept or taken for a release candidate. It matters most when the archive step is in <code>post { always }</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0306","card":"q-jenkins-069-jenkins-cli","sec":"jenkins-s070","q":"What is the Jenkins CLI, and how do you run management commands?","a":"<p><code>jenkins-cli.jar</code> runs admin and job commands from a terminal or script: create and build jobs, manage nodes, reload config, safe-restart. Authenticate with an API token, over SSH or the HTTP endpoint.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>java -jar jenkins-cli.jar -s https://jenkins.example.com -auth alice:TOKEN build my-job -f -v\njava -jar jenkins-cli.jar -s https://jenkins.example.com -auth alice:TOKEN safe-restart</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0307","card":"q-jenkins-069-jenkins-cli","sec":"jenkins-s070","q":"In Jenkins, how do you authenticate <code>jenkins-cli.jar</code> in a script, and why not use a password?","a":"<p>Use <code>-auth user:API_TOKEN</code> with a token that the user creates in their profile (Configure, API Token). A token can be revoked on its own, is limited to that user's rights and does not expose the login password. Store it in a credential and never in the script.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0308","card":"q-jenkins-069-jenkins-cli","sec":"jenkins-s070","q":"When would you use the Jenkins CLI and when the REST API?","a":"<p>The CLI is convenient for admin tasks from a shell: safe-restart, reload configuration, manage nodes, trigger builds and run the declarative linter. The REST API (JSON under <code>/api/json</code>, with <code>curl</code>) suits integrations from other tools and languages, since it needs only HTTP. Both are subject to the same authentication and permissions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0309","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"What is JCasC, and how does it replace manual UI administration?","a":"<p>The <code>configuration-as-code</code> plugin defines the whole controller configuration (security realm, clouds, tools, credentials, global settings) in a declarative YAML file. Instead of clicking through Manage Jenkins, you version the YAML and apply it, which makes the controller reproducible, reviewable and recoverable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jenkins:\n  systemMessage: &quot;Managed by JCasC&quot;\n  numExecutors: 0\n  securityRealm:\n    local: { allowsSignup: false }\n  authorizationStrategy:\n    roleBased: { roles: {} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0310","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"How do you export the existing configuration to <code>jenkins.yaml</code>?","a":"<p>Go to Manage Jenkins, Configuration as Code, View/Download Configuration to export the live config as YAML. Trim it, move secrets to environment variables or <code>${VAR}</code> references, commit it, and point <code>CASC_JENKINS_CONFIG</code> at it so later startups load it.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0311","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"What is Jenkins Configuration as Code (JCasC), and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>JCasC is the <code>configuration-as-code</code> plugin. It describes the controller's configuration (security realm, credentials, clouds, tools, global settings) in a YAML file that Jenkins applies at startup. It replaces manual clicking in Manage Jenkins, which is not reviewable and cannot be repeated exactly. With YAML in Git you get code review, history, easy rebuild after a failure and identical dev and prod controllers. The file's location is set with <code>CASC_JENKINS_CONFIG</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0312","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"What does JCasC not configure by itself, and how do you manage plugins in a reproducible controller?","a":"<p>JCasC sets up the configuration of installed plugins but does not install them. Keep a plugin list (a <code>plugins.txt</code>) and install it with <code>jenkins-plugin-cli</code> when you build the controller image. Then the image holds the right plugins and the YAML sets their configuration, which gives a fully reproducible controller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0313","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"How do you keep secrets out of a JCasC YAML file that is stored in Git?","a":"<p>Reference them as variables such as <code>${ADMIN_PASSWORD}</code>, resolved from environment variables, files or a secrets manager at startup. The YAML then contains only placeholders. Do not export live credentials into the file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0314","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"What happens to a setting that someone changes in the Jenkins UI when the controller is managed by JCasC?","a":"<p>The change can be overwritten the next time the YAML is applied (restart or reload), so the UI and the code drift apart. Treat the YAML as the source of truth: make changes through a reviewed commit, and consider locking down who may change configuration in the UI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0315","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"How can JCasC create jobs and not only global settings?","a":"<p>Combine it with the Job DSL plugin. A <code>jobs:</code> section in the YAML can hold Job DSL scripts that create folders and pipeline jobs at startup. Pipeline definitions themselves should stay in Jenkinsfiles in the repositories.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0316","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"How do you monitor controller health, CPU, memory and thread dumps?","a":"<p>Use the Monitoring (JavaMelody) plugin for CPU, heap and threads, the built-in System Information and Load Statistics, and take thread dumps at <code>/threadDump</code> (or via the CLI) to diagnose hangs. Watch JVM heap, GC, executor queue length and disk.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0317","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"How do you integrate Jenkins metrics with Prometheus and Grafana?","a":"<p>Install the Prometheus metrics plugin, which exposes <code>/prometheus</code> for scraping. Grafana dashboards then show queue length, build durations, executor utilization, node health and JVM stats. Add alerts for queue backlog or controller memory.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0318","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"How do you troubleshoot a hung or deadlocked Jenkins, or heavy GC pauses?","a":"<p>Capture thread dumps (<code>/threadDump</code>) to find blocked or deadlocked threads and heavy operations; capture a heap dump and enable GC logging to spot memory pressure. Common fixes: raise heap (<code>-Xmx</code>), tune GC (for example G1), reduce plugins and executors, move builds off the controller, add build discarders and split load across agents.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0319","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"Which Jenkins metrics would you alert on, and why?","a":"<p>Build queue length and wait time (shows a shortage of executors), executor utilization, node offline count, JVM heap usage and GC time (warns before an out-of-memory crash), disk space on <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> and failure rate of builds. Alert on trends and thresholds, not single events.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0320","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"Builds wait in the Jenkins queue while the controller looks idle. How do you decide whether you need more executors or a different fix?","a":"<p>Look at the queue's \"why\" text for each item. If all agents are busy, add executors or agents. If it says no node matches the label, fix labels or bring the node online. If a throttle, lock or quiet period holds the job, the limit is intentional. Also check offline agents, since a capacity problem can be a node problem.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0321","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"The Jenkins UI is very slow but builds run. What do you check first?","a":"<p>Check the controller's JVM: heap usage and GC pauses, thread dumps for stuck or blocked threads, and the number of plugins and jobs. Look at disk I/O on <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> and a long list of builds per job. Heavy plugins, large build histories and many polling jobs are common causes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0322","card":"q-jenkins-072-disk-hygiene-and-workspace-cleanup","sec":"jenkins-s073","q":"How do you manage disk space on the controller?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Build discarder</strong>: cap builds and artifacts kept per job (<code>buildDiscarder</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Artifact retention</strong>: keep artifacts for fewer builds than logs.</li>\n  <li>Clean workspaces (<code>cleanWs</code>), offload artifacts to external storage (Artifactory, Nexus, S3) and monitor <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> disk.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr: &#x27;30&#x27;, artifactNumToKeepStr: &#x27;5&#x27;)) }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0323","card":"q-jenkins-072-disk-hygiene-and-workspace-cleanup","sec":"jenkins-s073","q":"How do you clean workspaces after a build?","a":"<p>Use the Workspace Cleanup plugin's <code>cleanWs</code> step, typically in <code>post { always { } }</code>, which frees disk and avoids stale files contaminating later builds.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post { always { cleanWs() } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0324","card":"q-jenkins-072-disk-hygiene-and-workspace-cleanup","sec":"jenkins-s073","q":"In Jenkins, what is the difference between <code>numToKeepStr</code> and <code>daysToKeepStr</code> in <code>logRotator</code>, and which do you use?","a":"<p><code>numToKeepStr</code> keeps the last N builds, and <code>daysToKeepStr</code> keeps builds for N days. Builds are deleted when either limit is exceeded if both are set. A count is predictable for disk use. Add <code>artifactNumToKeepStr</code> to keep artifacts for fewer builds than logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0325","card":"q-jenkins-072-disk-hygiene-and-workspace-cleanup","sec":"jenkins-s073","q":"Why define <code>buildDiscarder</code> in the Jenkinsfile rather than in the job UI?","a":"<p>It is versioned with the pipeline, reviewed in pull requests, and applies to every job created from that repository, including new Multibranch branches. UI settings are per job, easy to forget and lost if the job is recreated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0326","card":"q-jenkins-072-disk-hygiene-and-workspace-cleanup","sec":"jenkins-s073","q":"In Jenkins, when would you clean the workspace before a build and not after it?","a":"<p>Clean before the build when you need a guaranteed fresh start but also want the files of the last run to stay available for debugging a failure. Cleaning after saves disk space immediately but removes evidence. Many teams clean at the start and rely on a periodic cleanup for disk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0327","card":"q-jenkins-073-quality-gates-and-vulnerability-scanning","sec":"jenkins-s074","q":"How do you integrate SonarQube and enforce quality gates?","a":"<p>Run the SonarScanner inside <code>withSonarQubeEnv</code>, then wait on the asynchronous quality gate with <code>waitForQualityGate</code> and fail the build if it is not passed.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;SonarQube&#x27;) {\n  steps { withSonarQubeEnv(&#x27;sonar&#x27;) { sh &#x27;mvn sonar:sonar&#x27; } }\n}\nstage(&#x27;Quality Gate&#x27;) {\n  steps { timeout(time: 10, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) {\n    waitForQualityGate abortPipeline: true    // fail if gate not met\n  } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0328","card":"q-jenkins-073-quality-gates-and-vulnerability-scanning","sec":"jenkins-s074","q":"How do you integrate vulnerability scanners (Trivy, Snyk, Dependency-Check)?","a":"<p>Add a security stage that runs the scanner and fails on threshold breaches (for example high or critical CVEs). Publish reports for visibility.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Scan&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    sh &#x27;trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity HIGH,CRITICAL registry/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;\n    // sh &#x27;snyk test --severity-threshold=high&#x27;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0329","card":"q-jenkins-073-quality-gates-and-vulnerability-scanning","sec":"jenkins-s074","q":"In Jenkins, why does <code>waitForQualityGate</code> need a webhook from SonarQube?","a":"<p>The scanner only uploads the analysis. SonarQube computes the quality gate result asynchronously afterwards. A webhook from SonarQube to Jenkins reports when the result is ready, so <code>waitForQualityGate</code> can finish without polling. Without that webhook the step waits until its timeout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0330","card":"q-jenkins-073-quality-gates-and-vulnerability-scanning","sec":"jenkins-s074","q":"In Jenkins, why wrap the Sonar scan in <code>withSonarQubeEnv('sonar')</code>?","a":"<p>It injects the server URL and the authentication token that were configured in Jenkins for that SonarQube installation as environment variables, so the scanner can connect without the Jenkinsfile containing the token.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0331","card":"q-jenkins-073-quality-gates-and-vulnerability-scanning","sec":"jenkins-s074","q":"What does <code>--exit-code 1</code> do in this Trivy command, and what happens without it?","a":"<p>With <code>--exit-code 1</code> Trivy exits with status 1 when it finds vulnerabilities of the listed severities, so the <code>sh</code> step fails and the pipeline stops. Without it Trivy exits with 0 even when it finds problems, so the stage would pass and the scan would only produce output. <code>--severity</code> limits the check to HIGH and CRITICAL.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sh &#x27;trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity HIGH,CRITICAL registry/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"jenkins-f0332","card":"q-jenkins-073-quality-gates-and-vulnerability-scanning","sec":"jenkins-s074","q":"What types of security checks fit into a CI pipeline, and what does each look at?","a":"<p>SAST scans the source code for insecure patterns. Dependency scanning (OWASP Dependency-Check, Snyk) looks for known vulnerabilities in libraries. Image scanning (Trivy) checks OS packages and libraries in a container image. Secret scanning looks for committed keys. DAST tests the running application. Run the fast ones early and on every commit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0333","card":"q-jenkins-074-notifications","sec":"jenkins-s075","q":"How do you send Slack, Teams or email notifications by outcome?","a":"<p>Put them in the <code>post</code> block keyed to result conditions, using the Slack, Teams or Email Extension plugins.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post {\n  success { slackSend channel: &#x27;#ci&#x27;, color: &#x27;good&#x27;,   message: &quot;✅ ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER}&quot; }\n  failure { slackSend channel: &#x27;#ci&#x27;, color: &#x27;danger&#x27;, message: &quot;❌ ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER} ${env.BUILD_URL}&quot; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0334","card":"q-jenkins-074-notifications","sec":"jenkins-s075","q":"In Jenkins, why does a notification with a <code>${env.JOB_NAME}</code> value need a double-quoted string?","a":"<p>Groovy interpolates <code>${...}</code> only in double-quoted strings. In a single-quoted string the text is sent literally as <code>${env.JOB_NAME}</code>. Use double quotes for messages that include variables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0335","card":"q-jenkins-074-notifications","sec":"jenkins-s075","q":"How do you avoid notification noise in Jenkins, so people do not ignore alerts?","a":"<p>Notify on state changes using the <code>changed</code> and <code>fixed</code> post conditions, plus <code>failure</code> for the team that owns the job. Avoid sending a message for every successful build. Include the job name, build number, link and the failing stage, and send them to a channel the owners watch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0336","card":"q-jenkins-074-notifications","sec":"jenkins-s075","q":"Where should a Slack token or webhook URL for Jenkins notifications be stored?","a":"<p>In the Jenkins credentials store, configured in the plugin's global settings or bound with <code>withCredentials</code>. The Jenkinsfile then holds no secret. A webhook URL is a secret too, because anyone who has it can post to the channel.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0337","card":"q-jenkins-075-scenario-controller-out-of-memory-from-p","sec":"jenkins-s076","q":"A commit triggers 10 parallel jobs and the controller crashes with OOM. How do you prevent and recover?","a":"<p>Recover by restarting the controller (temporarily with more <code>-Xmx</code>) and aborting the runaway builds. Prevent it by moving builds to agents (controller executors 0), capping concurrency with throttling, <code>disableConcurrentBuilds</code> and per-job or per-label limits, using a Quiet Period to coalesce rapid pushes, right-sizing executors, adding build discarders and alerting on heap and queue. Ephemeral Kubernetes agents keep load off the controller.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0338","card":"q-jenkins-075-scenario-controller-out-of-memory-from-p","sec":"jenkins-s076","q":"In Jenkins, how do the <code>lock</code> step and the Throttle Concurrent Builds plugin differ in limiting load?","a":"<p><code>lock</code> (Lockable Resources plugin) gives named locks to one build at a time, which suits a scarce thing such as a test environment. Throttle Concurrent Builds caps how many builds of a job, category or node run together. Use lock for exclusive resources and throttling to protect capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0339","card":"q-jenkins-075-scenario-controller-out-of-memory-from-p","sec":"jenkins-s076","q":"How do you get evidence about why the Jenkins JVM ran out of memory?","a":"<p>Start Jenkins with <code>-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError</code> and a dump path, so a heap dump is written at the crash. Analyze it with a tool such as Eclipse MAT to see what holds memory (often a plugin or large build histories). Add GC logs and heap monitoring to see the trend before the crash.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0340","card":"q-jenkins-076-scenario-a-pipeline-hangs-on-one-step","sec":"jenkins-s077","q":"A pipeline is stuck on a step indefinitely. How do you debug and safeguard?","a":"<p>Open the build's Console or Pipeline Steps to find the stuck step, take a controller thread dump (<code>/threadDump</code>) and check the agent (process, disk, network). Common culprits: a waiting <code>input</code>, a hung <code>sh</code> subprocess, network calls without timeouts, or an offline agent. Safeguard with <code>timeout</code> on risky steps, <code>retry</code> on flaky ones, an overall pipeline <code>timeout</code> option, and a timeout on <code>input</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { timeout(time: 60, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) }         // whole-pipeline guard\nsteps { timeout(time: 5, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./maybe-hangs.sh&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0341","card":"q-jenkins-076-scenario-a-pipeline-hangs-on-one-step","sec":"jenkins-s077","q":"In Jenkins, what does <code>timeout(time: 5, unit: 'MINUTES', activity: true)</code> do differently from a plain timeout?","a":"<p>With <code>activity: true</code> the timer is an inactivity timeout. It resets whenever the step produces log output, so the build is aborted only after five minutes with no output. A plain timeout stops the step after a fixed total time. Use inactivity for long but chatty tasks, and a fixed time as a hard upper bound.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0342","card":"q-jenkins-076-scenario-a-pipeline-hangs-on-one-step","sec":"jenkins-s077","q":"A Jenkins build does not stop when you click Abort. What can you do?","a":"<p>Aborting asks the build to stop, but a step stuck in a native call or a dead agent may ignore it. For Pipeline builds, a hard stop is available at the build's <code>term</code> and <code>kill</code> URLs (for example <code>/job/NAME/42/kill/</code>), which need administrator rights. Then find why it hung: thread dump, agent state and any waiting <code>input</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0343","card":"q-jenkins-077-scenario-helm-deploy-without-embedding-a","sec":"jenkins-s078","q":"How do you deploy to Kubernetes with Helm without cluster-admin credentials in the codebase?","a":"<p>Store the kubeconfig or token as a Jenkins credential (better, use a cloud IAM/OIDC identity or a Vault-issued short-lived token), bind it only for the deploy block with <code>withCredentials</code>, and use a least-privilege service account scoped to the target namespace rather than cluster-admin. Run the deploy on an ephemeral agent and never commit secrets.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withCredentials([file(credentialsId: &#x27;kubeconfig-prod&#x27;, variable: &#x27;KUBECONFIG&#x27;)]) {\n  sh &#x27;helm upgrade --install app ./chart -n app-prod --atomic --wait&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0344","card":"q-jenkins-077-scenario-helm-deploy-without-embedding-a","sec":"jenkins-s078","q":"What do <code>--atomic</code> and <code>--wait</code> do in <code>helm upgrade --install app ./chart -n app-prod --atomic --wait</code>?","a":"<p><code>--install</code> makes the command create the release if it does not exist and upgrade it otherwise. <code>--wait</code> makes Helm wait until the Kubernetes objects are ready. <code>--atomic</code> rolls the release back automatically if the upgrade fails or times out (and implies waiting), so the pipeline never leaves a half-deployed release.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0345","card":"q-jenkins-077-scenario-helm-deploy-without-embedding-a","sec":"jenkins-s078","q":"Why bind a kubeconfig as a file credential rather than writing it into the repository, and what should the kubeconfig allow?","a":"<p>A file credential is stored encrypted and placed in a temporary path only inside the <code>withCredentials</code> block, so the cluster access never enters Git or logs. The kubeconfig should use a service account limited to the target namespace and the verbs it needs for deploys, so a leak affects only that namespace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0346","card":"q-jenkins-077-scenario-helm-deploy-without-embedding-a","sec":"jenkins-s078","q":"What is better than a long-lived kubeconfig token for a Jenkins deploy job to a cloud Kubernetes cluster?","a":"<p>A short-lived identity: the agent's cloud IAM role or workload identity (for example IRSA on EKS), or a token issued per deploy by Vault. The credential expires by itself, so a leak is far less harmful than a static admin token.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0347","card":"q-jenkins-078-scenario-migrating-200-freestyle-jobs-to","sec":"jenkins-s079","q":"What is the step-by-step strategy?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Inventory and categorize jobs by pattern (build, deploy, cron).</li>\n  <li>Build templates and Shared Library steps for each common pattern.</li>\n  <li>Pilot-migrate a few representative jobs to Jenkinsfiles in their repos and run them in parallel with the old jobs to compare.</li>\n  <li>Roll out by category, converting configs to declarative plus Multibranch.</li>\n  <li>Standardize credentials through the credentials store and parameterize jobs.</li>\n  <li>Decommission Freestyle jobs once the pipelines are verified.</li>\n  <li>Add JCasC so the setup is reproducible.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Migrate incrementally, never big-bang.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0348","card":"q-jenkins-078-scenario-migrating-200-freestyle-jobs-to","sec":"jenkins-s079","q":"Which parts of a Freestyle job configuration map to which parts of a Declarative Pipeline?","a":"<p>SCM settings map to <code>checkout scm</code> or a Multibranch source. Build triggers map to the <code>triggers</code> block or webhooks. Build steps map to <code>stages</code> and <code>steps</code>. Build wrappers and environment settings map to <code>options</code> and <code>environment</code>. Post-build actions map to the <code>post</code> section. Parameters map to the <code>parameters</code> block.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0349","card":"q-jenkins-078-scenario-migrating-200-freestyle-jobs-to","sec":"jenkins-s079","q":"Why run old Freestyle jobs and new pipelines side by side during a migration?","a":"<p>You can compare results, durations and artifacts of the same commit, and keep delivering with the old job if the new one has a gap. Switch over only when the outputs match, then retire the old job. This reduces the risk compared with replacing everything at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0350","card":"q-jenkins-078-scenario-migrating-200-freestyle-jobs-to","sec":"jenkins-s079","q":"How can you speed up migrating many similar jobs?","a":"<p>Find the common patterns and put them in a Shared Library step or a template Jenkinsfile, so each repository's file is a few lines. Bulk-read the existing <code>config.xml</code> files to classify jobs, and use Job DSL or Multibranch to create the new jobs automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0351","card":"q-jenkins-079-scenario-blue-green-or-canary-deployment","sec":"jenkins-s080","q":"How do you design a zero-downtime blue/green or canary pipeline?","a":"<p><strong>Blue/green</strong>: deploy the new version to an idle green environment, run smoke tests, then switch the load balancer or service from blue to green (rollback is switching back). <strong>Canary</strong>: route a small percentage of traffic to the new version, watch metrics and error rates, then ramp up or roll back. In Jenkins use stages for deploy-green, test, <code>input</code> approval (or an automated metric gate), traffic shift and verify, with automatic rollback on failure.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Switch traffic&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    sh &#x27;kubectl apply -f green-deploy.yaml &amp;&amp; ./smoke-test.sh green&#x27;\n    input message: &#x27;Cut over to green?&#x27;, ok: &#x27;Switch&#x27;\n    sh &#x27;kubectl patch service app -p \\&#x27;{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;selector&quot;:{&quot;slot&quot;:&quot;green&quot;}}}\\&#x27;&#x27;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0352","card":"q-jenkins-079-scenario-blue-green-or-canary-deployment","sec":"jenkins-s080","q":"How do blue/green, canary and rolling deployments differ?","a":"<p>Blue/green keeps two full environments and switches all traffic at once, with a quick rollback by switching back but double the capacity cost. Canary sends a small share of traffic to the new version first and grows it while watching metrics, which limits the impact of a bad release. Rolling replaces instances a few at a time, using little extra capacity, but both versions run together and rollback is slower.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0353","card":"q-jenkins-079-scenario-blue-green-or-canary-deployment","sec":"jenkins-s080","q":"What makes database changes hard in a blue/green deployment, and how do you handle them?","a":"<p>Both versions may use the same database, so a schema change must work for the old and the new code. Use the expand and contract pattern: first add new columns or tables compatibly, deploy the code, and remove the old structures in a later release. Avoid destructive changes in the same release as the switch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0354","card":"q-jenkins-079-scenario-blue-green-or-canary-deployment","sec":"jenkins-s080","q":"Why must a smoke test run against the green environment before traffic is switched?","a":"<p>It proves that the new version starts and answers basic requests while no user is affected, so a bad build is caught before cutover. The check should be automated, and the pipeline should stop and keep traffic on blue if it fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0355","card":"q-jenkins-080-scenario-flaky-scm-checkout","sec":"jenkins-s081","q":"How do you make checkout robust against intermittent network failures?","a":"<p>Wrap <code>checkout</code> in <code>retry</code> and <code>timeout</code>, log each attempt, and use <code>catchError</code> or <code>try</code> to surface diagnostics. Shallow clones and reasonable Git timeouts also reduce flakiness.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Checkout&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    retry(3) {\n      timeout(time: 5, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) {\n        echo &quot;Checkout attempt at ${new Date()}&quot;\n        checkout scm\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0356","card":"q-jenkins-080-scenario-flaky-scm-checkout","sec":"jenkins-s081","q":"Why is retrying a Git checkout not a fix for the root cause, and when should you not retry?","a":"<p>Retry hides intermittent failures but does not remove them, so it should be paired with logging and a look at the cause (network, rate limits, an overloaded Git server). Do not retry authentication or permission errors or a missing branch, since they fail every time and only waste time. Retry only transient errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0357","card":"q-jenkins-080-scenario-flaky-scm-checkout","sec":"jenkins-s081","q":"How can you cut checkout time and failures for a large repository in Jenkins?","a":"<p>Use a shallow clone (limited depth) and sparse checkout if only part of the repository is needed, use a reference repository or a mirror close to the agents, keep a persistent workspace or cache on static agents, and set a checkout timeout. Use webhooks instead of polling to reduce load on the Git server.</p>","hand":true}];
